1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,760

2
00:00:05,760 --> 00:00:06,260
Hey, guys.

3
00:00:06,260 --> 00:00:11,030
Welcome to our last
lecture of this quarter.

4
00:00:11,030 --> 00:00:15,350
And we're very happy
to have Douwe here.

5
00:00:15,350 --> 00:00:20,450
He's the CEO of Contextual AI,
the enterprise LLM company,

6
00:00:20,450 --> 00:00:23,450
as well as an adjunct
professor in symbolic systems

7
00:00:23,450 --> 00:00:24,680
here at Stanford.

8
00:00:24,680 --> 00:00:27,860
And previously, he was the head
of research at Hugging Face;

9
00:00:27,860 --> 00:00:31,100
and before that, a research
scientist at Facebook AI

10
00:00:31,100 --> 00:00:32,299
Research.

11
00:00:32,299 --> 00:00:35,977
He received his PhD and master's
from the University of Cambridge

12
00:00:35,977 --> 00:00:38,060
as well as a master's in
logic from the University

13
00:00:38,060 --> 00:00:41,090
of Amsterdam and studied
philosophy and cognitive

14
00:00:41,090 --> 00:00:42,770
AI in undergrad.

15
00:00:42,770 --> 00:00:46,430
And his work focuses on machine
learning, as well as NLP,

16
00:00:46,430 --> 00:00:49,010
specifically on developing
better models for language

17
00:00:49,010 --> 00:00:53,240
understanding and generation,
and better tools for evaluation

18
00:00:53,240 --> 00:00:54,690
and management.

19
00:00:54,690 --> 00:00:56,930
OK, I give it up for Douwe.

20
00:00:56,930 --> 00:00:59,250
All right.

21
00:00:59,250 --> 00:01:00,160
Thank you.

22
00:01:00,160 --> 00:01:02,790
So I guess I have to
stand here in the corner

23
00:01:02,790 --> 00:01:06,060
so people can see me
on the Zoom as well.

24
00:01:06,060 --> 00:01:10,230
Yeah, thanks so much
for having me here.

25
00:01:10,230 --> 00:01:12,270
So I asked Stephen what
I should talk about.

26
00:01:12,270 --> 00:01:15,060
There were a couple of things I
could talk about-- multimodality

27
00:01:15,060 --> 00:01:16,710
or evaluation.

28
00:01:16,710 --> 00:01:20,010
And this was the
preferred topic, I guess,

29
00:01:20,010 --> 00:01:23,160
because the others
were already covered.

30
00:01:23,160 --> 00:01:26,040
So yeah, I'm very happy to
talk to you about everything

31
00:01:26,040 --> 00:01:28,590
retrieval augmentation.

32
00:01:28,590 --> 00:01:30,690
I think this is really
one of the coolest topics

33
00:01:30,690 --> 00:01:32,820
right now in our field.

34
00:01:32,820 --> 00:01:34,500
So I'll just give
you an overview

35
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:36,210
of what's been
happening and what

36
00:01:36,210 --> 00:01:40,600
I think are the interesting
questions to think about.

37
00:01:40,600 --> 00:01:43,410
So first of all, obviously,
in case you've missed it,

38
00:01:43,410 --> 00:01:46,200
we are in the age
of language models.

39
00:01:46,200 --> 00:01:48,870
And I just wanted to
do a quick poll here

40
00:01:48,870 --> 00:01:50,803
in this not super big audience.

41
00:01:50,803 --> 00:01:52,470
I guess there's more
people on the Zoom.

42
00:01:52,470 --> 00:01:56,570
But who invented
language models?

43
00:01:56,570 --> 00:02:00,740
If you thought OpenAI,
then I'm angry with you.

44
00:02:00,740 --> 00:02:04,230
So actually, this is
a very, very old idea.

45
00:02:04,230 --> 00:02:06,320
So the idea is just
you take a sequence,

46
00:02:06,320 --> 00:02:09,560
and you vectorize out
the token probabilities.

47
00:02:09,560 --> 00:02:12,830
And so it wasn't
invented by OpenAI.

48
00:02:12,830 --> 00:02:14,690
It's not like a few years old.

49
00:02:14,690 --> 00:02:17,535
It's actually
several decades' old.

50
00:02:17,535 --> 00:02:19,910
So I'm bringing this up because
I was talking to someone,

51
00:02:19,910 --> 00:02:22,280
and they were like OpenAI
invented language models.

52
00:02:22,280 --> 00:02:25,220
And I was like, you're
kidding me, right?

53
00:02:25,220 --> 00:02:28,177
So I went back to
the literature.

54
00:02:28,177 --> 00:02:30,260
And this is the oldest one
I could find, actually.

55
00:02:30,260 --> 00:02:33,380
1991, first neural
language model.

56
00:02:33,380 --> 00:02:37,430
There's a very nice paper
from 2003 from Bengio,

57
00:02:37,430 --> 00:02:40,310
where they actually have word
embeddings and everything

58
00:02:40,310 --> 00:02:41,840
already in there.

59
00:02:41,840 --> 00:02:45,530
So, obviously, these
are LMs, not LLMs.

60
00:02:45,530 --> 00:02:48,170
And as it turns out, if
you make them really big

61
00:02:48,170 --> 00:02:51,417
and you parameterize them with
these massive neural nets,

62
00:02:51,417 --> 00:02:53,000
then you get something
really powerful

63
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:55,640
that really shows
emergent properties.

64
00:02:55,640 --> 00:02:59,430
And that's why we're all
so excited in this stuff.

65
00:02:59,430 --> 00:03:03,000
So if we think about this
from a classic CS perspective,

66
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:04,290
there's input/output.

67
00:03:04,290 --> 00:03:06,090
There's this thing
in the middle.

68
00:03:06,090 --> 00:03:07,200
It's the generator.

69
00:03:07,200 --> 00:03:10,290
So we take a sequence,
the input sequence.

70
00:03:10,290 --> 00:03:14,190
And then the task of the model
is to predict the next token.

71
00:03:14,190 --> 00:03:16,800
Very, very simple model.

72
00:03:16,800 --> 00:03:20,970
And so that's why it was so easy
to come up with this in 1991

73
00:03:20,970 --> 00:03:23,700
already, because the
idea is very intuitive.

74
00:03:23,700 --> 00:03:26,760
But for a long time, what
was really broken with

75
00:03:26,760 --> 00:03:29,310
this was the user interface.

76
00:03:29,310 --> 00:03:32,160
And this, I think,
a lot of people

77
00:03:32,160 --> 00:03:34,800
misunderstand what
ChatGPT was about.

78
00:03:34,800 --> 00:03:37,140
That's really what
ChatGPT fixed.

79
00:03:37,140 --> 00:03:40,980
So initially, you had to come
up with these very weird prompts

80
00:03:40,980 --> 00:03:42,540
in order to get
your language model

81
00:03:42,540 --> 00:03:44,610
to do what you wanted it to do.

82
00:03:44,610 --> 00:03:46,740
And humans are terrible at this.

83
00:03:46,740 --> 00:03:50,280
So we're much better at telling
people or things around us

84
00:03:50,280 --> 00:03:51,120
what we want.

85
00:03:51,120 --> 00:03:52,800
So if we have a dog, we say sit.

86
00:03:52,800 --> 00:03:56,630
We don't prompt it in a very
weird way so that it sits.

87
00:03:56,630 --> 00:03:58,380
And it's the same with
the language model.

88
00:03:58,380 --> 00:04:00,840
If you wanted to
generate some rap

89
00:04:00,840 --> 00:04:04,200
lyrics in the style of a pirate
or Shakespeare or something,

90
00:04:04,200 --> 00:04:05,910
then you tell it,
generate some rap

91
00:04:05,910 --> 00:04:08,040
lyrics in the style of pirates.

92
00:04:08,040 --> 00:04:10,530
So that kind of
instruction data actually

93
00:04:10,530 --> 00:04:14,190
turns out to be super,
super rare in just web data.

94
00:04:14,190 --> 00:04:16,890
So what you need to do is you
need to fix the user interface

95
00:04:16,890 --> 00:04:17,970
to the language model.

96
00:04:17,970 --> 00:04:22,110
And the classic recipe for doing
that is the sequence basically

97
00:04:22,110 --> 00:04:23,390
the ChatGPT used.

98
00:04:23,390 --> 00:04:25,140
So you prompt the model
in a specific way.

99
00:04:25,140 --> 00:04:26,640
You instruction
fine tune the model,

100
00:04:26,640 --> 00:04:31,657
and you do some alignment RLHF,
whatever you do on top of that.

101
00:04:31,657 --> 00:04:32,740
So that's the first thing.

102
00:04:32,740 --> 00:04:34,960
So now you have a
working language model

103
00:04:34,960 --> 00:04:37,510
with a working user interface.

104
00:04:37,510 --> 00:04:40,000
So are we done then?

105
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:41,440
Obviously, we're not.

106
00:04:41,440 --> 00:04:45,010
So right now, language models
are taking the world by storm.

107
00:04:45,010 --> 00:04:47,920
But if you talk to anyone,
especially in an enterprise,

108
00:04:47,920 --> 00:04:50,950
for example, where they
have very strict accuracy

109
00:04:50,950 --> 00:04:53,350
requirements, they will tell
you that they can't really

110
00:04:53,350 --> 00:04:55,213
productionize this yet.

111
00:04:55,213 --> 00:04:57,880
And the reason is because there
are all these familiar problems.

112
00:04:57,880 --> 00:05:00,088
Probably a bunch of you are
working on these problems

113
00:05:00,088 --> 00:05:03,190
right now around hallucination.

114
00:05:03,190 --> 00:05:05,320
So these models,
they make up stuff

115
00:05:05,320 --> 00:05:07,210
very often with very
high confidence, which

116
00:05:07,210 --> 00:05:10,570
is even more scary in a way.

117
00:05:10,570 --> 00:05:12,850
Attribution-- so we don't
really know why these models

118
00:05:12,850 --> 00:05:14,740
are saying what they're saying.

119
00:05:14,740 --> 00:05:16,510
Stillness-- they go out of date.

120
00:05:16,510 --> 00:05:18,850
So this was a big
problem with ChatGPT

121
00:05:18,850 --> 00:05:21,380
not knowing anything that
happened after a certain cutoff

122
00:05:21,380 --> 00:05:21,880
date.

123
00:05:21,880 --> 00:05:23,920
And they keep updating
it every once in a while.

124
00:05:23,920 --> 00:05:25,420
But you want to
have a system that's

125
00:05:25,420 --> 00:05:28,840
always completely up to
date, that never goes stale.

126
00:05:28,840 --> 00:05:31,520
You want to be able to revise
the information in the system.

127
00:05:31,520 --> 00:05:34,640
So if you're a
European organization,

128
00:05:34,640 --> 00:05:36,793
you have to worry
about GDPR, which

129
00:05:36,793 --> 00:05:38,960
means that you need to be
able to remove information

130
00:05:38,960 --> 00:05:42,410
from the language model or
maybe revise facts, which

131
00:05:42,410 --> 00:05:43,910
we don't really know how to do.

132
00:05:43,910 --> 00:05:46,940
So again, this is a
very interesting area

133
00:05:46,940 --> 00:05:50,240
of study for a lot of
folks model editing.

134
00:05:50,240 --> 00:05:53,180
So this is something that we
really want to be able to fix.

135
00:05:53,180 --> 00:05:55,250
And then there's
this big question of,

136
00:05:55,250 --> 00:05:57,710
how do you customize
these models?

137
00:05:57,710 --> 00:05:59,840
So different people have
different use cases.

138
00:05:59,840 --> 00:06:00,860
You have different data.

139
00:06:00,860 --> 00:06:02,390
If you're a company
or if you want

140
00:06:02,390 --> 00:06:04,280
to have a language
model on your own data,

141
00:06:04,280 --> 00:06:06,500
how do you make it
work on your own data?

142
00:06:06,500 --> 00:06:10,130
So one of the solutions
that everybody

143
00:06:10,130 --> 00:06:12,140
has started using right
now is to couple it

144
00:06:12,140 --> 00:06:13,280
to an external memory.

145
00:06:13,280 --> 00:06:15,290
So that's really just RAG.

146
00:06:15,290 --> 00:06:19,550
So we can-- this whole lecture
is basically about RAG.

147
00:06:19,550 --> 00:06:22,910
But the way to understand
what is going on here

148
00:06:22,910 --> 00:06:25,740
is we have a generator
just like before.

149
00:06:25,740 --> 00:06:27,740
We have the input and a
prompt just like before.

150
00:06:27,740 --> 00:06:30,860
But now, instead of just
giving those two things,

151
00:06:30,860 --> 00:06:32,720
we give this additional context.

152
00:06:32,720 --> 00:06:37,220
So we contextualize the language
model using things we retrieved.

153
00:06:37,220 --> 00:06:40,670
And the retriever is
very often pretty simple.

154
00:06:40,670 --> 00:06:43,190
It's just a query and
a document encoder.

155
00:06:43,190 --> 00:06:45,350
And then you get a
bunch of documents.

156
00:06:45,350 --> 00:06:47,720
You give them as context
through the model.

157
00:06:47,720 --> 00:06:50,870
So super simple architecture.

158
00:06:50,870 --> 00:06:54,020
And I think it's useful
to think about it

159
00:06:54,020 --> 00:06:57,680
from the perspective of
these two separate paradigms.

160
00:06:57,680 --> 00:06:59,420
So if you've ever
taken an exam--

161
00:06:59,420 --> 00:07:01,077
I'm sure you have--

162
00:07:01,077 --> 00:07:02,660
you can have a closed
book exam, where

163
00:07:02,660 --> 00:07:04,285
you have to memorize
all of this or you

164
00:07:04,285 --> 00:07:07,040
have to cram all the knowledge
into your parameters,

165
00:07:07,040 --> 00:07:08,390
your neurons.

166
00:07:08,390 --> 00:07:09,980
Or you have an open
book exam, where

167
00:07:09,980 --> 00:07:12,020
you have all of this
information in the book

168
00:07:12,020 --> 00:07:14,750
that you can access
when you do the exam.

169
00:07:14,750 --> 00:07:16,732
So it's a very similar
thing with RAG.

170
00:07:16,732 --> 00:07:19,190
You can just make it an open
book setting where you give it

171
00:07:19,190 --> 00:07:22,010
access to this external
information Wikipedia

172
00:07:22,010 --> 00:07:25,400
or something else or
basically the entire internet

173
00:07:25,400 --> 00:07:27,110
and then have the
language model do

174
00:07:27,110 --> 00:07:29,150
its job without having
to memorize all of it

175
00:07:29,150 --> 00:07:31,650
in its parameters.

176
00:07:31,650 --> 00:07:33,560
So the other, I think,
useful distinction

177
00:07:33,560 --> 00:07:37,070
here is that cramming everything
into your parameters, that's

178
00:07:37,070 --> 00:07:38,660
the parametric approach.

179
00:07:38,660 --> 00:07:41,150
So what we're doing
with RAG is we're

180
00:07:41,150 --> 00:07:44,090
adding this non-parametric
retrieval component.

181
00:07:44,090 --> 00:07:47,300
So you might call
this semi-parametric

182
00:07:47,300 --> 00:07:50,510
if you want to give this a name.

183
00:07:50,510 --> 00:07:54,620
All right, so why does that
actually solve these issues?

184
00:07:54,620 --> 00:07:57,380
And so the answer is
basically that if you

185
00:07:57,380 --> 00:08:00,470
have this separate index,
this separate retriever,

186
00:08:00,470 --> 00:08:01,490
you can swap it in.

187
00:08:01,490 --> 00:08:02,330
You can swap it out.

188
00:08:02,330 --> 00:08:04,460
You can replace it
with a new index

189
00:08:04,460 --> 00:08:06,210
so you can really customize it.

190
00:08:06,210 --> 00:08:09,110
And so you can customize
your language model system

191
00:08:09,110 --> 00:08:12,462
for what the user
really wants to see.

192
00:08:12,462 --> 00:08:14,420
And then, obviously, you
can update this index.

193
00:08:14,420 --> 00:08:16,880
So it doesn't really
go stale, and you

194
00:08:16,880 --> 00:08:21,270
can revise it if everything goes
wrong-- if anything goes wrong.

195
00:08:21,270 --> 00:08:23,400
The other thing you
get is grounding.

196
00:08:23,400 --> 00:08:25,735
So that's initially
why I became interested

197
00:08:25,735 --> 00:08:27,360
in this kind of
architecture, because I

198
00:08:27,360 --> 00:08:29,550
was thinking a lot about
grounding and multimodality

199
00:08:29,550 --> 00:08:30,425
and things like that.

200
00:08:30,425 --> 00:08:32,820
And actually, one really
nice way to ground things

201
00:08:32,820 --> 00:08:35,159
is to find some other
information that you

202
00:08:35,159 --> 00:08:36,780
can ground your generation in.

203
00:08:36,780 --> 00:08:39,840
So you really want the language
model to only say things

204
00:08:39,840 --> 00:08:43,500
that it has evidence for
in this other piece of text

205
00:08:43,500 --> 00:08:46,350
or even multimodal data that
it retrieves separately.

206
00:08:46,350 --> 00:08:48,508
So if you do that, then
you get less hallucination,

207
00:08:48,508 --> 00:08:50,550
because you can always
point back to your source.

208
00:08:50,550 --> 00:08:52,849
It's always grounded
in your source.

209
00:08:52,849 --> 00:08:54,307
And you get
attribution because you

210
00:08:54,307 --> 00:08:56,182
know why the model is
saying what it's saying

211
00:08:56,182 --> 00:08:58,830
is because it found
that this thing here.

212
00:08:58,830 --> 00:09:02,030
Is that clear?

213
00:09:02,030 --> 00:09:05,450
All right, so for the
rest of this lecture,

214
00:09:05,450 --> 00:09:08,750
we're going to talk about
this basic architecture.

215
00:09:08,750 --> 00:09:12,590
And so it kind looks like
a pretty simple thing,

216
00:09:12,590 --> 00:09:14,180
but there are
actually lots and lots

217
00:09:14,180 --> 00:09:17,390
of questions you can ask about
what this system should really

218
00:09:17,390 --> 00:09:18,650
look like.

219
00:09:18,650 --> 00:09:22,790
And this doesn't even cover
half the questions you can ask.

220
00:09:22,790 --> 00:09:27,777
So it really is about how do
we optimize this entire system.

221
00:09:27,777 --> 00:09:29,360
So we have these
separate components--

222
00:09:29,360 --> 00:09:31,220
the retriever, the generator.

223
00:09:31,220 --> 00:09:34,610
And then there are things
like this query encoder.

224
00:09:34,610 --> 00:09:35,900
How do we encode queries?

225
00:09:35,900 --> 00:09:38,030
How do we do the retrieval?

226
00:09:38,030 --> 00:09:40,220
Do we update the
document encoder?

227
00:09:40,220 --> 00:09:43,400
How do we actually
define the document?

228
00:09:43,400 --> 00:09:44,840
Is it like a full
document, or is

229
00:09:44,840 --> 00:09:48,410
it a paragraph or a chunk or a
sentence or a couple of words?

230
00:09:48,410 --> 00:09:50,540
So there are lots
of questions to ask.

231
00:09:50,540 --> 00:09:54,650
And as you'll see, there
are lots of possible answers

232
00:09:54,650 --> 00:09:56,550
to these questions as well.

233
00:09:56,550 --> 00:10:00,650
So this is what we'll cover.

234
00:10:00,650 --> 00:10:04,490
So there are lots
of architectures

235
00:10:04,490 --> 00:10:05,990
going into these questions.

236
00:10:05,990 --> 00:10:09,560
And I think as we go through
them, it's useful for you

237
00:10:09,560 --> 00:10:12,020
to think about what happens
during training time

238
00:10:12,020 --> 00:10:14,400
and what happens
during test time.

239
00:10:14,400 --> 00:10:16,858
So during training time,
it's really, OK, we

240
00:10:16,858 --> 00:10:17,900
have this language model.

241
00:10:17,900 --> 00:10:20,270
We have this retriever.

242
00:10:20,270 --> 00:10:21,740
Which one do we update?

243
00:10:21,740 --> 00:10:22,910
How do we update them?

244
00:10:22,910 --> 00:10:24,740
How do we train
this entire system?

245
00:10:24,740 --> 00:10:26,870
Do we maybe not train it at all?

246
00:10:26,870 --> 00:10:28,460
Do we pre-train it from scratch?

247
00:10:28,460 --> 00:10:31,730
Do we initialize it with
components that were already

248
00:10:31,730 --> 00:10:32,752
separately trained?

249
00:10:32,752 --> 00:10:34,460
These are the kinds
of questions that you

250
00:10:34,460 --> 00:10:37,700
have to answer if you want
to design a system like this.

251
00:10:37,700 --> 00:10:41,630
And then during test time,
you have this entire system.

252
00:10:41,630 --> 00:10:47,540
So actually multiple models in
a way that are working together.

253
00:10:47,540 --> 00:10:49,700
So there's also different
things you can do there.

254
00:10:49,700 --> 00:10:51,980
So give it different
indices during test time

255
00:10:51,980 --> 00:10:56,370
or manipulate how you're
sampling, things like that.

256
00:10:56,370 --> 00:10:59,700
So the starting point
for all of this stuff,

257
00:10:59,700 --> 00:11:02,100
I think if you ask someone
now, like, What is RAG?

258
00:11:02,100 --> 00:11:04,470
they will think of this thing.

259
00:11:04,470 --> 00:11:08,130
So this is frozen
RAG, basically.

260
00:11:08,130 --> 00:11:09,700
There's no training here at all.

261
00:11:09,700 --> 00:11:12,120
So going back to this question
of train time, test time,

262
00:11:12,120 --> 00:11:13,380
there's only test time here.

263
00:11:13,380 --> 00:11:16,440
Train time happens separately
with these black box

264
00:11:16,440 --> 00:11:19,020
models that we don't
necessarily have control over.

265
00:11:19,020 --> 00:11:21,900
So there's this
document embedding model

266
00:11:21,900 --> 00:11:23,790
is whatever is
currently at the top

267
00:11:23,790 --> 00:11:26,790
of some open-source leaderboard.

268
00:11:26,790 --> 00:11:29,130
You use that-- oops, sorry--

269
00:11:29,130 --> 00:11:32,100
to get some vectors
that you then use

270
00:11:32,100 --> 00:11:33,930
to create this vector database.

271
00:11:33,930 --> 00:11:36,160
And then the vector
database just does search.

272
00:11:36,160 --> 00:11:39,280
And it gives the information
from the search to the language

273
00:11:39,280 --> 00:11:39,780
model.

274
00:11:39,780 --> 00:11:43,500
And it just passes
it as the context.

275
00:11:43,500 --> 00:11:47,730
So this only works because
of in-context learning.

276
00:11:47,730 --> 00:11:51,840
And I think as a
machine learner myself,

277
00:11:51,840 --> 00:11:54,120
this feels very inelegant.

278
00:11:54,120 --> 00:11:56,340
So what this
lecture is about is,

279
00:11:56,340 --> 00:12:01,110
can we do better than
this frozen thing?

280
00:12:01,110 --> 00:12:05,220
So let's start from
the left side of this.

281
00:12:05,220 --> 00:12:07,920
OK, if we want to outperform
this frozen thing itself

282
00:12:07,920 --> 00:12:09,990
with just the vector
database, what

283
00:12:09,990 --> 00:12:14,030
would that look like from
a retrieval perspective?

284
00:12:14,030 --> 00:12:18,020
And the starting point for
everything retrieval is TF-IDF.

285
00:12:18,020 --> 00:12:21,140
Does everybody know
what TF-IDF is?

286
00:12:21,140 --> 00:12:21,640
No?

287
00:12:21,640 --> 00:12:26,320
OK, so TF-IDF is basically
a sparse retrieval method,

288
00:12:26,320 --> 00:12:29,350
where you have a
score function that

289
00:12:29,350 --> 00:12:31,570
looks at documents and queries.

290
00:12:31,570 --> 00:12:35,230
So D and Q. And then there are
basically two terms that matter.

291
00:12:35,230 --> 00:12:37,210
One is the TF, the
Term Frequency.

292
00:12:37,210 --> 00:12:40,420
And the other is the IDF, the
Inverse Document Frequency.

293
00:12:40,420 --> 00:12:42,070
So this inverse
document frequency

294
00:12:42,070 --> 00:12:45,100
is actually a really nice
idea from Karen Sparck Jones,

295
00:12:45,100 --> 00:12:46,480
really underrated researcher.

296
00:12:46,480 --> 00:12:48,760
She's done some amazing work.

297
00:12:48,760 --> 00:12:51,250
But the basic idea
is that you want

298
00:12:51,250 --> 00:12:53,500
to look at the words
that are very special.

299
00:12:53,500 --> 00:12:55,840
So they don't occur in lots
of different documents.

300
00:12:55,840 --> 00:12:58,840
And so the overlap between
the word "the" doesn't really

301
00:12:58,840 --> 00:13:00,070
matter.

302
00:13:00,070 --> 00:13:01,450
"The" occurs everywhere.

303
00:13:01,450 --> 00:13:03,730
So you want to have
the special words.

304
00:13:03,730 --> 00:13:06,760
So that's what TF-IDF does
in a nutshell-- it gives you

305
00:13:06,760 --> 00:13:09,730
a score for document
query overlap.

306
00:13:09,730 --> 00:13:12,130
And then you can do
all kinds of things

307
00:13:12,130 --> 00:13:13,652
here with how you weight it.

308
00:13:13,652 --> 00:13:15,610
So there's all these
weird different parameters

309
00:13:15,610 --> 00:13:17,480
like this b and
things like that,

310
00:13:17,480 --> 00:13:20,470
that allow you to make
it better than just

311
00:13:20,470 --> 00:13:22,400
having the TF-IDF score.

312
00:13:22,400 --> 00:13:24,410
So there's a couple of
tweaks you can do there.

313
00:13:24,410 --> 00:13:27,140
So BM25, actually, in
case you're wondering,

314
00:13:27,140 --> 00:13:29,360
stands for Best Match 25.

315
00:13:29,360 --> 00:13:33,860
So I tried to discover where
does the 25 actually come from.

316
00:13:33,860 --> 00:13:39,140
That's because the preceding
24 experiments failed.

317
00:13:39,140 --> 00:13:41,570
So it's literally the 25th
one that seemed to work,

318
00:13:41,570 --> 00:13:44,030
and that's why it's called BM25.

319
00:13:44,030 --> 00:13:46,700
It's bizarre.

320
00:13:46,700 --> 00:13:48,800
So this is sparse retrieval.

321
00:13:48,800 --> 00:13:50,060
It's just counting words.

322
00:13:50,060 --> 00:13:52,130
So you have this
massive, massive vector

323
00:13:52,130 --> 00:13:53,960
of all these word occurrences.

324
00:13:53,960 --> 00:13:56,450
It's sparse because
most words never occur.

325
00:13:56,450 --> 00:14:02,120
So it's sort of like a vector
of vocabulary size dimensions.

326
00:14:02,120 --> 00:14:05,240
So most of that
is obviously zero.

327
00:14:05,240 --> 00:14:07,160
So that's actually
a nice property

328
00:14:07,160 --> 00:14:09,770
if you want to do
fast search on a CPU.

329
00:14:09,770 --> 00:14:12,500
Because on a CPU,
sparse dot product

330
00:14:12,500 --> 00:14:14,240
is fairly easy to compute.

331
00:14:14,240 --> 00:14:20,030
So this is used in the system
called DrQA, which is really

332
00:14:20,030 --> 00:14:24,230
one of the first neural
instances of this open domain,

333
00:14:24,230 --> 00:14:27,260
open book question
answering paradigm.

334
00:14:27,260 --> 00:14:30,590
So you have a question like,
how many of Warsaw's inhabitants

335
00:14:30,590 --> 00:14:31,700
blah, blah, blah.

336
00:14:31,700 --> 00:14:34,163
So you want to ask
basically Wikipedia

337
00:14:34,163 --> 00:14:35,330
what the answer is for this.

338
00:14:35,330 --> 00:14:37,130
So then you have this
document retriever

339
00:14:37,130 --> 00:14:39,080
based on these sparse--

340
00:14:39,080 --> 00:14:41,630
so BM25, I think, in this case--

341
00:14:41,630 --> 00:14:42,740
retrieval methods.

342
00:14:42,740 --> 00:14:45,530
You pass that to this--

343
00:14:45,530 --> 00:14:49,220
I think this was still
by LSTM at the time--

344
00:14:49,220 --> 00:14:50,690
a document reader model.

345
00:14:50,690 --> 00:14:54,140
And then that model
gives you the answer.

346
00:14:54,140 --> 00:14:56,540
So this, I think, is
really the first instance

347
00:14:56,540 --> 00:14:59,270
of having this separation
between a retrieval

348
00:14:59,270 --> 00:15:01,880
and a generator
system that you use

349
00:15:01,880 --> 00:15:05,060
for answering complicated
questions based on open domain

350
00:15:05,060 --> 00:15:07,100
knowledge.

351
00:15:07,100 --> 00:15:11,090
So after the sparse
stuff, there was a bunch

352
00:15:11,090 --> 00:15:12,650
of work on dense retrieval.

353
00:15:12,650 --> 00:15:15,380
And so the advantage
of dense retrieval--

354
00:15:15,380 --> 00:15:18,290
so this is just like word
embeddings, basically vectors.

355
00:15:18,290 --> 00:15:20,300
They're dense now,
no longer sparse.

356
00:15:20,300 --> 00:15:23,890
So they're much smaller in
terms of dimensionality.

357
00:15:23,890 --> 00:15:26,740
And the nice advantage
of dense retrieval

358
00:15:26,740 --> 00:15:28,960
is that it's not really
about specific words.

359
00:15:28,960 --> 00:15:32,860
So if there are
synonyms, you can still

360
00:15:32,860 --> 00:15:36,010
find the relevant document,
which you couldn't really do

361
00:15:36,010 --> 00:15:37,720
with a sparse representation.

362
00:15:37,720 --> 00:15:39,700
So that's really the
advantage of dense

363
00:15:39,700 --> 00:15:43,150
is that you get
semantic similarity.

364
00:15:43,150 --> 00:15:45,815
So you can do this
over word embeddings

365
00:15:45,815 --> 00:15:47,440
that doesn't really
work all that well.

366
00:15:47,440 --> 00:15:50,230
But at the time that people
started thinking about this,

367
00:15:50,230 --> 00:15:51,400
BERT was already out there.

368
00:15:51,400 --> 00:15:53,140
And BERT is really
great for giving you

369
00:15:53,140 --> 00:15:56,290
a vector representation for
an entire sequence of words.

370
00:15:56,290 --> 00:15:59,320
So a sentence representation
or a passage representation.

371
00:15:59,320 --> 00:16:03,100
So there are all these cool
systems like ORCA and DPR,

372
00:16:03,100 --> 00:16:07,780
the Dense Passage Retriever,
where they essentially

373
00:16:07,780 --> 00:16:11,620
use the retrieval as a latent
variable in the system.

374
00:16:11,620 --> 00:16:13,810
And the way to get
the latent variable

375
00:16:13,810 --> 00:16:16,420
to work to be good
enough, essentially

376
00:16:16,420 --> 00:16:19,060
to train the entire
system is to pre-train

377
00:16:19,060 --> 00:16:21,680
the retriever on
relevant information.

378
00:16:21,680 --> 00:16:25,640
So for archive, they do
something called inverse close.

379
00:16:25,640 --> 00:16:28,010
So they do a close
task where you

380
00:16:28,010 --> 00:16:32,450
want to find passages that
are relevant to the preceding

381
00:16:32,450 --> 00:16:33,350
passage.

382
00:16:33,350 --> 00:16:36,590
And then DPR, they just train
it on the supervised thing.

383
00:16:36,590 --> 00:16:38,210
But really, the
core idea here is

384
00:16:38,210 --> 00:16:40,730
that, as you can see
in this graph here,

385
00:16:40,730 --> 00:16:44,600
you can do better than BM25
if you had lots of documents.

386
00:16:44,600 --> 00:16:47,180
And the way you compute the
score function is much simpler.

387
00:16:47,180 --> 00:16:48,263
It's just the dot product.

388
00:16:48,263 --> 00:16:50,690

389
00:16:50,690 --> 00:16:53,690
So the nice thing
about dot products

390
00:16:53,690 --> 00:16:57,170
is that you can do them very,
very efficiently on the GPU

391
00:16:57,170 --> 00:17:00,660
as well if you know
what you're doing.

392
00:17:00,660 --> 00:17:02,900
So what you really
want to get at

393
00:17:02,900 --> 00:17:05,190
is Maximum Inner
Product Search, MIPS.

394
00:17:05,190 --> 00:17:08,990
So this is one of the core
ideas of a lot of this stuff.

395
00:17:08,990 --> 00:17:13,310
And you can do MIPS with ANN,
Approximate Nearest Neighbor

396
00:17:13,310 --> 00:17:14,490
search.

397
00:17:14,490 --> 00:17:17,960
And so there's this really
brilliant piece of work

398
00:17:17,960 --> 00:17:20,960
out of FAIR for my
colleagues at the time

399
00:17:20,960 --> 00:17:23,210
called FAISS, which
really underlies

400
00:17:23,210 --> 00:17:26,089
all of these modern
vector databases.

401
00:17:26,089 --> 00:17:29,450
So all the popular ones that
are of re-implementations

402
00:17:29,450 --> 00:17:32,078
of this FAISS idea one is
in like Rust one, is in Go.

403
00:17:32,078 --> 00:17:33,620
But it's all basically
the same idea.

404
00:17:33,620 --> 00:17:35,930
It's just FAISS.

405
00:17:35,930 --> 00:17:39,380
And so FAISS really powers
a lot of this stuff.

406
00:17:39,380 --> 00:17:41,600
And whenever somebody
tells you something

407
00:17:41,600 --> 00:17:44,000
about a vector database,
just think about FAISS.

408
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:45,425
Very fast dot product.

409
00:17:45,425 --> 00:17:47,970

410
00:17:47,970 --> 00:17:50,970
So, obviously, you can
go beyond dot product.

411
00:17:50,970 --> 00:17:51,780
Yes.

412
00:17:51,780 --> 00:17:52,650
What is it?

413
00:17:52,650 --> 00:17:53,430
What's FAISS?

414
00:17:53,430 --> 00:17:54,540
What is FAISS?

415
00:17:54,540 --> 00:17:55,530
Yeah.

416
00:17:55,530 --> 00:17:58,935
So it's an open-source library,
Facebook AI Similarity Search.

417
00:17:58,935 --> 00:18:02,990
Yeah, [INAUDIBLE].

418
00:18:02,990 --> 00:18:06,590
No, so it's just basically
off the shelf and algorithms.

419
00:18:06,590 --> 00:18:10,510

420
00:18:10,510 --> 00:18:13,090
Yeah, so there are all
kinds of different--

421
00:18:13,090 --> 00:18:15,670
I don't know if you do you know
what product quantization is

422
00:18:15,670 --> 00:18:16,790
and things like that.

423
00:18:16,790 --> 00:18:20,200
So there are basically-- so
you have a bunch of vectors.

424
00:18:20,200 --> 00:18:23,200
And you can just compute
the full dot product,

425
00:18:23,200 --> 00:18:24,850
which is sort of inefficient.

426
00:18:24,850 --> 00:18:29,290
So what you can do is try to
compress subspaces of the vector

427
00:18:29,290 --> 00:18:33,550
and then just look at
the kind of centroids.

428
00:18:33,550 --> 00:18:36,490
So you can quantize
subvectors of the full vector

429
00:18:36,490 --> 00:18:39,670
and then do much faster search
over just the centroids.

430
00:18:39,670 --> 00:18:42,035

431
00:18:42,035 --> 00:18:42,910
It's a good question.

432
00:18:42,910 --> 00:18:43,960
Any other questions?

433
00:18:43,960 --> 00:18:47,520

434
00:18:47,520 --> 00:18:50,880
All right, so about
this dot product idea.

435
00:18:50,880 --> 00:18:53,610
So what we have
here is some people

436
00:18:53,610 --> 00:18:55,200
call this a Siamese network.

437
00:18:55,200 --> 00:18:55,890
I guess it is.

438
00:18:55,890 --> 00:18:59,640
So you have two different
bird models or whatever

439
00:18:59,640 --> 00:19:00,515
your encoder is here.

440
00:19:00,515 --> 00:19:02,473
And then at the end, you
get these two vectors.

441
00:19:02,473 --> 00:19:03,900
And then you just
do dot product.

442
00:19:03,900 --> 00:19:05,550
So you get one single score.

443
00:19:05,550 --> 00:19:08,010
But you can do all kinds
of much fancier things

444
00:19:08,010 --> 00:19:13,170
if you're willing to give up
on this bi encoder approach.

445
00:19:13,170 --> 00:19:15,930
So a really nice example
from one of your colleagues

446
00:19:15,930 --> 00:19:19,290
here at Stanford is ColBert.

447
00:19:19,290 --> 00:19:23,250
So what this does is
lay the interaction.

448
00:19:23,250 --> 00:19:25,680
So instead of just having
this dot product here,

449
00:19:25,680 --> 00:19:30,570
you have a more complicated
version of computing a score

450
00:19:30,570 --> 00:19:33,090
where you aggregate
over maximum similarity

451
00:19:33,090 --> 00:19:34,860
scores between different words.

452
00:19:34,860 --> 00:19:36,900
So I only recently
actually discovered

453
00:19:36,900 --> 00:19:39,540
that this is called ColBert
because of the late night show

454
00:19:39,540 --> 00:19:40,320
Colbert.

455
00:19:40,320 --> 00:19:43,560
So it's Omar's joke,
actually, this name.

456
00:19:43,560 --> 00:19:46,650
But just so you know,
if you run into it.

457
00:19:46,650 --> 00:19:50,140

458
00:19:50,140 --> 00:19:53,640
But I think if we look at
where the state of the art

459
00:19:53,640 --> 00:19:56,890
has been going now, one of the
nice things about these vector

460
00:19:56,890 --> 00:19:58,930
databases is that
they're super efficient.

461
00:19:58,930 --> 00:20:00,760
So dot product is
much more efficient

462
00:20:00,760 --> 00:20:02,200
than this late
interaction stuff,

463
00:20:02,200 --> 00:20:04,480
especially if you do the
approximate nearest neighbor

464
00:20:04,480 --> 00:20:05,740
search.

465
00:20:05,740 --> 00:20:07,810
But there's been some
really cool work.

466
00:20:07,810 --> 00:20:11,740
So things like
SPLADE, they basically

467
00:20:11,740 --> 00:20:14,560
have sparse meat dense in a way.

468
00:20:14,560 --> 00:20:16,660
So one of the big problems
as I said with sparse

469
00:20:16,660 --> 00:20:19,300
is that you can't really handle
synonyms and things like that.

470
00:20:19,300 --> 00:20:23,230
But what you could do is take a
dense model like a BERT model,

471
00:20:23,230 --> 00:20:26,620
look at this one word
in your sequence,

472
00:20:26,620 --> 00:20:29,380
try to see which other
words fit in the same slot.

473
00:20:29,380 --> 00:20:31,570
So that gives you the synonyms.

474
00:20:31,570 --> 00:20:35,860
So now you can give all these
synonyms to a sparse vector.

475
00:20:35,860 --> 00:20:37,930
And then you can just
do sparse dot product,

476
00:20:37,930 --> 00:20:41,800
and so have a much, much more
efficient way to do search

477
00:20:41,800 --> 00:20:45,400
without giving up on
all the cool stuff

478
00:20:45,400 --> 00:20:48,010
that you get from a
dense representation.

479
00:20:48,010 --> 00:20:49,090
So that's one thing.

480
00:20:49,090 --> 00:20:53,680
And this other idea I really
like is called DRAGON.

481
00:20:53,680 --> 00:20:57,040
So this, I think, is
really the best generalized

482
00:20:57,040 --> 00:20:57,820
dense retriever.

483
00:20:57,820 --> 00:20:59,840
So if you want to take something
off the shelf right now

484
00:20:59,840 --> 00:21:01,600
and just go to Hugging
Face or something,

485
00:21:01,600 --> 00:21:04,240
then this DRAGON or
DragonPlus is probably

486
00:21:04,240 --> 00:21:06,430
the thing you want to
use for dense retriever.

487
00:21:06,430 --> 00:21:10,300
And the way they train this is
through this progressive data

488
00:21:10,300 --> 00:21:13,060
augmentation strategy to make
the model better and better

489
00:21:13,060 --> 00:21:16,300
over time by sampling
very difficult negatives.

490
00:21:16,300 --> 00:21:20,530
And that gives you very
good representations.

491
00:21:20,530 --> 00:21:22,660
And so the other
thing about this

492
00:21:22,660 --> 00:21:27,070
I think is the only final point
about retrieval in general

493
00:21:27,070 --> 00:21:29,920
is that what we see happening
right now if you look

494
00:21:29,920 --> 00:21:32,080
at the developer
community around RAG

495
00:21:32,080 --> 00:21:34,850
is that they're all doing
hybrid search right now.

496
00:21:34,850 --> 00:21:37,180
So you can actually just
combine the search results

497
00:21:37,180 --> 00:21:41,110
from your sparse BM25 or
whatever thing or SPLAYED.

498
00:21:41,110 --> 00:21:43,840
And you can combine
them with your DRAGON.

499
00:21:43,840 --> 00:21:47,050
And then you get this ranking
that works even better.

500
00:21:47,050 --> 00:21:48,650
So then you get
best of both worlds,

501
00:21:48,650 --> 00:21:50,150
but then you get
all these questions

502
00:21:50,150 --> 00:21:53,900
about how do you
combine the results.

503
00:21:53,900 --> 00:21:57,140
Any questions on this part?

504
00:21:57,140 --> 00:21:58,980
Oh, can you hear me?

505
00:21:58,980 --> 00:21:59,790
Yes.

506
00:21:59,790 --> 00:22:00,990
Oh, sorry.

507
00:22:00,990 --> 00:22:03,780
On the earlier
slide, has there been

508
00:22:03,780 --> 00:22:08,190
any work on benchmark, how much
less hallucination RAG incurs

509
00:22:08,190 --> 00:22:10,950
over a closed book
question answering?

510
00:22:10,950 --> 00:22:13,560
For example, directly asking
the large language model

511
00:22:13,560 --> 00:22:17,280
the question, has there been any
benchmarking studies in this?

512
00:22:17,280 --> 00:22:20,640
Yeah, so there's a great
paper if I can say so myself

513
00:22:20,640 --> 00:22:22,560
on the fact that
retrieval augmentation

514
00:22:22,560 --> 00:22:24,480
reduces hallucination.

515
00:22:24,480 --> 00:22:27,210
It's from 2021, I think.

516
00:22:27,210 --> 00:22:28,680
So yeah, you can just--

517
00:22:28,680 --> 00:22:31,530
if you literally look for
retrieval augmentation reduces

518
00:22:31,530 --> 00:22:34,030
hallucination, then
you'll find the paper.

519
00:22:34,030 --> 00:22:34,780
Oh, thank you.

520
00:22:34,780 --> 00:22:37,732

521
00:22:37,732 --> 00:22:41,035
[INAUDIBLE],, what's
the [INAUDIBLE]??

522
00:22:41,035 --> 00:22:43,990

523
00:22:43,990 --> 00:22:51,220
Yeah, so very often, you want to
have very precise word overlap

524
00:22:51,220 --> 00:22:53,800
for things where you don't
want to have the synonyms

525
00:22:53,800 --> 00:22:55,270
or the nearest neighbors.

526
00:22:55,270 --> 00:23:01,150
So if there's a brand name or
something like that, then--

527
00:23:01,150 --> 00:23:02,530
let's say the brand is Apple.

528
00:23:02,530 --> 00:23:04,930
You don't want to find
stuff about pears.

529
00:23:04,930 --> 00:23:08,740
So that's what you would
do with a dense retriever.

530
00:23:08,740 --> 00:23:12,100
So it really depends on
what you want to use it for.

531
00:23:12,100 --> 00:23:15,920
That's why hybrid is
probably the way to go.

532
00:23:15,920 --> 00:23:17,890
It's a good question.

533
00:23:17,890 --> 00:23:23,980
Wait, with the dense things,
it's contextualized embeddings.

534
00:23:23,980 --> 00:23:26,830
But shouldn't it realize
Apple, the company,

535
00:23:26,830 --> 00:23:28,060
would be different from--

536
00:23:28,060 --> 00:23:31,450
No, so if they were actually
contextualized, then yes.

537
00:23:31,450 --> 00:23:34,960
But very often, it's a
frozen retrieval system.

538
00:23:34,960 --> 00:23:37,735
That's one of the problems
with all the frozen RAG stuff.

539
00:23:37,735 --> 00:23:42,330

540
00:23:42,330 --> 00:23:44,252
I might be missing
something very basic here.

541
00:23:44,252 --> 00:23:47,180
[INAUDIBLE] referring
to the vectors

542
00:23:47,180 --> 00:23:49,370
that you're getting
the [INAUDIBLE]..

543
00:23:49,370 --> 00:23:51,830
Vectors that you're using is
either the [INAUDIBLE] query

544
00:23:51,830 --> 00:23:54,930
or the [INAUDIBLE].

545
00:23:54,930 --> 00:23:55,590
No.

546
00:23:55,590 --> 00:24:01,260
And so the document and the
query, they're the same.

547
00:24:01,260 --> 00:24:03,750
So they're either
sparse, or they're dense.

548
00:24:03,750 --> 00:24:06,420
So if they're sparse, the
components of the vector

549
00:24:06,420 --> 00:24:09,500
are literally the other words.

550
00:24:09,500 --> 00:24:10,000
So--

551
00:24:10,000 --> 00:24:11,980
You just one
penalized when you're

552
00:24:11,980 --> 00:24:17,950
[INAUDIBLE] to the thing
that creates the embeddings?

553
00:24:17,950 --> 00:24:19,700
How are you getting [INAUDIBLE]?

554
00:24:19,700 --> 00:24:22,420
So it's literally counts.

555
00:24:22,420 --> 00:24:26,650
So basically, it's one big
matrix of documents as rows.

556
00:24:26,650 --> 00:24:29,230
And the columns are the
words in the documents.

557
00:24:29,230 --> 00:24:30,760
And then you just
count how often

558
00:24:30,760 --> 00:24:32,650
a word occurs in a document.

559
00:24:32,650 --> 00:24:33,190
Oh.

560
00:24:33,190 --> 00:24:34,330
So that's a sparse.

561
00:24:34,330 --> 00:24:36,340
[INAUDIBLE] property of sparse.

562
00:24:36,340 --> 00:24:40,430
You're also referring
to [INAUDIBLE]..

563
00:24:40,430 --> 00:24:42,860
Yeah, and so in
the field, we call

564
00:24:42,860 --> 00:24:46,310
them sparse embeddings
or sparse retrieval

565
00:24:46,310 --> 00:24:50,810
because most of that vector
is 0 because most words don't

566
00:24:50,810 --> 00:24:54,220
occur in that document.

567
00:24:54,220 --> 00:24:55,320
Does that make sense?

568
00:24:55,320 --> 00:24:55,820
Yeah.

569
00:24:55,820 --> 00:24:58,640

570
00:24:58,640 --> 00:25:00,300
Cool.

571
00:25:00,300 --> 00:25:05,000
So let's talk about
doing slightly better.

572
00:25:05,000 --> 00:25:06,950
So going back to
Steven's question about,

573
00:25:06,950 --> 00:25:08,870
OK, we have this
retrieval thing.

574
00:25:08,870 --> 00:25:12,890
But how do we actually make this
retrieval good for the context

575
00:25:12,890 --> 00:25:14,480
that it's going to be used in?

576
00:25:14,480 --> 00:25:18,170
So can we contextualize the
retrieval for the generator,

577
00:25:18,170 --> 00:25:20,975
even if it's a generator
where we might not

578
00:25:20,975 --> 00:25:22,100
have access to the weights?

579
00:25:22,100 --> 00:25:24,080
So it could be a GPT4 model.

580
00:25:24,080 --> 00:25:25,610
We just send it to some API.

581
00:25:25,610 --> 00:25:27,890
We get some stuff back.

582
00:25:27,890 --> 00:25:32,300
And so one paper I really
like is called "RePlug."

583
00:25:32,300 --> 00:25:35,100
So just to explain
what this looks like.

584
00:25:35,100 --> 00:25:36,200
So you have this context.

585
00:25:36,200 --> 00:25:39,330
You have a retriever that
we do the standard retrieval

586
00:25:39,330 --> 00:25:39,830
step with.

587
00:25:39,830 --> 00:25:42,620
This is a dense retriever.

588
00:25:42,620 --> 00:25:43,730
Sorry.

589
00:25:43,730 --> 00:25:46,880
And now you compute
the likelihood.

590
00:25:46,880 --> 00:25:48,890
So you basically just
normalize the scores

591
00:25:48,890 --> 00:25:51,290
that you get for
the top k documents

592
00:25:51,290 --> 00:25:53,030
to get a distribution here.

593
00:25:53,030 --> 00:25:56,900
And then you give each one
of the retrieved documents

594
00:25:56,900 --> 00:26:00,570
separately to this generator,
to your language model.

595
00:26:00,570 --> 00:26:04,260
So you can look at the
perplexity of the correct answer

596
00:26:04,260 --> 00:26:06,270
for that language model.

597
00:26:06,270 --> 00:26:09,360
So now we have these two
probability distributions or two

598
00:26:09,360 --> 00:26:10,410
likelihoods, essentially.

599
00:26:10,410 --> 00:26:12,720
And we can minimize
the kl divergence

600
00:26:12,720 --> 00:26:14,610
to make sure that
we can actually

601
00:26:14,610 --> 00:26:17,370
retrieve the documents
that lead to the lowest

602
00:26:17,370 --> 00:26:21,330
perplexity on the right
answer for the language model.

603
00:26:21,330 --> 00:26:23,860
So super simple idea.

604
00:26:23,860 --> 00:26:26,400
Works really, really well.

605
00:26:26,400 --> 00:26:29,940
And the nice thing about this
is completely agnostic of what

606
00:26:29,940 --> 00:26:31,110
happens upstream.

607
00:26:31,110 --> 00:26:33,630
So this will work for any
sort of encoder-decoder.

608
00:26:33,630 --> 00:26:38,880
For any language model, what
you need is a perplexity score.

609
00:26:38,880 --> 00:26:40,740
But for most
language models, you

610
00:26:40,740 --> 00:26:43,020
can get that, not
necessarily all of them.

611
00:26:43,020 --> 00:26:44,070
So that's one thing.

612
00:26:44,070 --> 00:26:48,360
And then there's this other
really nice approach on--

613
00:26:48,360 --> 00:26:52,320
Well, what parameters are
[INAUDIBLE] in the retriever?

614
00:26:52,320 --> 00:26:54,270
So in the retriever,
you're literally

615
00:26:54,270 --> 00:26:58,440
updating the dense
representations.

616
00:26:58,440 --> 00:27:01,080
So your encoder basically for
your dense representation.

617
00:27:01,080 --> 00:27:02,038
That's a good question.

618
00:27:02,038 --> 00:27:04,460
We'll get into that a bit more.

619
00:27:04,460 --> 00:27:08,630
So there's another paper on
in-context retrieval augmented

620
00:27:08,630 --> 00:27:11,720
language models, where the
whole paper is basically

621
00:27:11,720 --> 00:27:15,470
about just doing BM25 and
just giving stuff directly

622
00:27:15,470 --> 00:27:17,030
to the context of
the language model.

623
00:27:17,030 --> 00:27:17,960
And things work.

624
00:27:17,960 --> 00:27:22,490
So it's frozen RAG but even
more primitive in a way

625
00:27:22,490 --> 00:27:26,930
where the retriever is this
very old sparse algorithm.

626
00:27:26,930 --> 00:27:28,880
But it works
really, really well.

627
00:27:28,880 --> 00:27:31,310
But then they have this
really awesome section

628
00:27:31,310 --> 00:27:35,510
where they show that you can
just have this reranker on top

629
00:27:35,510 --> 00:27:37,880
of the BM25 results.

630
00:27:37,880 --> 00:27:40,160
And you can backprop
into this reranker.

631
00:27:40,160 --> 00:27:43,220
So now you still keep the
language model completely fixed.

632
00:27:43,220 --> 00:27:46,700
So that's this part
of the loss here.

633
00:27:46,700 --> 00:27:49,430
So you have a stop gradient
on the parameters theta.

634
00:27:49,430 --> 00:27:51,080
That's just your language model.

635
00:27:51,080 --> 00:27:54,800
But now you have
this rank function

636
00:27:54,800 --> 00:27:56,960
here that you can backprop into.

637
00:27:56,960 --> 00:27:58,850
So that's your
reranker is basically--

638
00:27:58,850 --> 00:28:00,783
it can be a BERT model
or anything like that

639
00:28:00,783 --> 00:28:02,450
works on top of the
things you initially

640
00:28:02,450 --> 00:28:04,160
retrieved from your BM25.

641
00:28:04,160 --> 00:28:06,050
And now you have
this BERT reranker

642
00:28:06,050 --> 00:28:08,720
that you can backprop into.

643
00:28:08,720 --> 00:28:11,360
So this also works
really, really nice.

644
00:28:11,360 --> 00:28:14,630
So we're slowly progressing
towards having a system that

645
00:28:14,630 --> 00:28:19,250
is much more optimized for being
properly retrieval augmented

646
00:28:19,250 --> 00:28:22,040
in a way where it's useful
and contextualized for what

647
00:28:22,040 --> 00:28:24,650
you want to use it for.

648
00:28:24,650 --> 00:28:27,290
So yeah, just to point
out what that looks

649
00:28:27,290 --> 00:28:28,310
like with this reranker.

650
00:28:28,310 --> 00:28:30,680
So you just have this
extra step, essentially.

651
00:28:30,680 --> 00:28:33,050
So we have our retriever,
then we have our reranker,

652
00:28:33,050 --> 00:28:36,820
then we have our
generator and our output.

653
00:28:36,820 --> 00:28:39,910
[INAUDIBLE]

654
00:28:39,910 --> 00:28:43,570
No, not necessarily.

655
00:28:43,570 --> 00:28:45,670
So for this one, you do, yeah.

656
00:28:45,670 --> 00:28:48,800
So for RePlug, you don't.

657
00:28:48,800 --> 00:28:50,160
Yeah, [INAUDIBLE].

658
00:28:50,160 --> 00:28:50,660
Got it.

659
00:28:50,660 --> 00:28:52,010
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

660
00:28:52,010 --> 00:28:53,720
So basically, you need to get--

661
00:28:53,720 --> 00:28:55,400
[INAUDIBLE]

662
00:28:55,400 --> 00:28:57,170
Not all of them.

663
00:28:57,170 --> 00:28:58,438
Some of them do.

664
00:28:58,438 --> 00:29:00,230
But yeah, there are
all kinds of tricks you

665
00:29:00,230 --> 00:29:01,460
can do on top of that, yeah.

666
00:29:01,460 --> 00:29:04,310

667
00:29:04,310 --> 00:29:07,760
So basically, the
question is, how do we get

668
00:29:07,760 --> 00:29:09,290
gradients flowing into this?

669
00:29:09,290 --> 00:29:11,240
So if you don't
actually have access

670
00:29:11,240 --> 00:29:12,870
to the full parameters
of the model

671
00:29:12,870 --> 00:29:14,870
so that you can backprop
all the way through it,

672
00:29:14,870 --> 00:29:20,090
then you can do a reinforce
style loss on the retrieval.

673
00:29:20,090 --> 00:29:22,670
And then you just pass
the log likelihood

674
00:29:22,670 --> 00:29:26,045
if you have access to that or
some other black box function.

675
00:29:26,045 --> 00:29:31,550

676
00:29:31,550 --> 00:29:35,930
All right, the next
thing, you can do

677
00:29:35,930 --> 00:29:40,100
is to optimize both the
retriever and the generator.

678
00:29:40,100 --> 00:29:43,070
And so this really
starts getting

679
00:29:43,070 --> 00:29:46,670
to the proper contextualization
of the entire architecture

680
00:29:46,670 --> 00:29:48,785
where you want everything
to work together.

681
00:29:48,785 --> 00:29:50,660
So rather than having
this frozen thing where

682
00:29:50,660 --> 00:29:52,730
everything is
basically not aware

683
00:29:52,730 --> 00:29:53,953
that the other part exists.

684
00:29:53,953 --> 00:29:55,370
It's like two
halves of the brain.

685
00:29:55,370 --> 00:29:56,787
They're not talking
to each other.

686
00:29:56,787 --> 00:29:57,770
One is your retriever.

687
00:29:57,770 --> 00:29:58,970
That is your language model.

688
00:29:58,970 --> 00:29:59,887
There's no connection.

689
00:29:59,887 --> 00:30:01,430
They're just sort
of like something

690
00:30:01,430 --> 00:30:03,920
is thrown over the fence, and
then you hope for the best.

691
00:30:03,920 --> 00:30:06,350
So instead of that, we
have everything much closer

692
00:30:06,350 --> 00:30:08,660
and learning together.

693
00:30:08,660 --> 00:30:14,810
So one of the first ways of
doing this with a generator

694
00:30:14,810 --> 00:30:17,330
was RAG, Retrieval
Augmented Generation,

695
00:30:17,330 --> 00:30:20,180
which we did at FAIR in 2020.

696
00:30:20,180 --> 00:30:23,840
And it's very similar to
what we've already seen.

697
00:30:23,840 --> 00:30:25,700
We basically have
this retriever here

698
00:30:25,700 --> 00:30:27,440
that works over
different documents.

699
00:30:27,440 --> 00:30:29,780
You get some score
function that gets

700
00:30:29,780 --> 00:30:33,620
given to this generator
that generates the answer.

701
00:30:33,620 --> 00:30:35,600
And now you want to
backprop all the way

702
00:30:35,600 --> 00:30:38,060
and update your
generator as well.

703
00:30:38,060 --> 00:30:40,010
So in the previous
two architectures,

704
00:30:40,010 --> 00:30:41,930
we saw you keep the
generator fixed.

705
00:30:41,930 --> 00:30:44,510
You backprop into
your retriever.

706
00:30:44,510 --> 00:30:46,400
But here we update everything.

707
00:30:46,400 --> 00:30:48,440
Well, not exactly
everything, as you'll see,

708
00:30:48,440 --> 00:30:52,280
but we'll also update
the part of the retriever

709
00:30:52,280 --> 00:30:54,320
and the generator.

710
00:30:54,320 --> 00:30:57,470
So in this RAG
model, we actually

711
00:30:57,470 --> 00:30:59,220
have two different
ways of doing this.

712
00:30:59,220 --> 00:31:01,610
And this is probably
something that when

713
00:31:01,610 --> 00:31:05,150
we talk about this, if you think
about this long enough, then

714
00:31:05,150 --> 00:31:09,350
you'll think like, OK, but when
actually do I need to retrieve?

715
00:31:09,350 --> 00:31:12,170
Do I retrieve every time
I generate a new token?

716
00:31:12,170 --> 00:31:14,690
Or do I just retrieve
once and then generate

717
00:31:14,690 --> 00:31:16,730
an entire sequence?

718
00:31:16,730 --> 00:31:20,502
Or maybe I want to
retrieve every n tokens?

719
00:31:20,502 --> 00:31:21,710
So these are hyperparameters.

720
00:31:21,710 --> 00:31:23,420
Or maybe I want to
learn when to retrieve

721
00:31:23,420 --> 00:31:26,720
as well as we'll see that's
also something people have done.

722
00:31:26,720 --> 00:31:29,810
So these are two
different ways to do it.

723
00:31:29,810 --> 00:31:32,580
And what we do in this
paper-- basically,

724
00:31:32,580 --> 00:31:35,460
the whole point of the paper is
that this frozen thing doesn't

725
00:31:35,460 --> 00:31:37,440
really work all that well.

726
00:31:37,440 --> 00:31:41,730
So I think what people
call RAG now is usually

727
00:31:41,730 --> 00:31:44,970
refers to the frozen thing,
but the whole paper basically

728
00:31:44,970 --> 00:31:47,220
would never have been accepted
anywhere if we had just

729
00:31:47,220 --> 00:31:48,300
done the frozen thing.

730
00:31:48,300 --> 00:31:52,420
The whole point of the paper is
that you want to optimize it.

731
00:31:52,420 --> 00:31:54,690
And so at my
company, Contextual,

732
00:31:54,690 --> 00:31:57,090
we call this frozen thing
Frankenstein's monster,

733
00:31:57,090 --> 00:31:58,950
because it's really
like you cobble together

734
00:31:58,950 --> 00:32:01,380
these different pieces.

735
00:32:01,380 --> 00:32:02,760
It's really like Frankenstein.

736
00:32:02,760 --> 00:32:05,843
You just put it together,
and then it walks.

737
00:32:05,843 --> 00:32:07,260
But it doesn't
really have a soul.

738
00:32:07,260 --> 00:32:08,790
It doesn't really actually work.

739
00:32:08,790 --> 00:32:10,860
It's not the real thing.

740
00:32:10,860 --> 00:32:13,765
So that's great for
everyone here, I think,

741
00:32:13,765 --> 00:32:15,390
because there are so
many opportunities

742
00:32:15,390 --> 00:32:20,100
to do better than what most
people are using right now.

743
00:32:20,100 --> 00:32:24,650
So one of the limitations of
the original RAG architecture

744
00:32:24,650 --> 00:32:27,560
is that it only
supports a very small k.

745
00:32:27,560 --> 00:32:30,740
So if you have lots
and lots of documents,

746
00:32:30,740 --> 00:32:33,320
then the problem is that
you have to fit all of them

747
00:32:33,320 --> 00:32:34,040
into context.

748
00:32:34,040 --> 00:32:37,850
But how do you really
get that to fit?

749
00:32:37,850 --> 00:32:43,340
So one thing you can do is
you first encode things so

750
00:32:43,340 --> 00:32:46,250
that you get one single
representation or only

751
00:32:46,250 --> 00:32:48,200
the few top-level
representations.

752
00:32:48,200 --> 00:32:49,160
Then you concatenate.

753
00:32:49,160 --> 00:32:51,590
Those and then you just
feed them to the decoder.

754
00:32:51,590 --> 00:32:54,770
So this is FiD,
Fusion in Decoder.

755
00:32:54,770 --> 00:32:58,540
And as you can see, this
scales to a much higher number

756
00:32:58,540 --> 00:33:03,170
of passages, and that leads
to corresponding improvements

757
00:33:03,170 --> 00:33:07,210
in the scores that
you care about.

758
00:33:07,210 --> 00:33:08,530
So that's a really cool idea.

759
00:33:08,530 --> 00:33:11,790
And so we're slowly
moving towards more

760
00:33:11,790 --> 00:33:13,530
decoder-only architectures.

761
00:33:13,530 --> 00:33:15,270
So in RAG, we have
this BART model.

762
00:33:15,270 --> 00:33:17,340
It's an encoder-decoder
architecture.

763
00:33:17,340 --> 00:33:18,900
But here, you just
have this decoder

764
00:33:18,900 --> 00:33:22,020
that does some fancy
attention over stuff

765
00:33:22,020 --> 00:33:24,450
that you retrieved before.

766
00:33:24,450 --> 00:33:31,200
And so another pure decoder
language model architecture

767
00:33:31,200 --> 00:33:34,050
is this one, kNN-LM,
which I think

768
00:33:34,050 --> 00:33:36,660
is very elegant
in its simplicity.

769
00:33:36,660 --> 00:33:39,600
So it's basically you just
have a normal language model.

770
00:33:39,600 --> 00:33:43,710
But you interpolate the
normal language model weights

771
00:33:43,710 --> 00:33:46,740
with things that you retrieved.

772
00:33:46,740 --> 00:33:49,290
So basically, you have
some sort of prompt.

773
00:33:49,290 --> 00:33:51,510
So like Obama's birthplace is.

774
00:33:51,510 --> 00:33:52,890
You go to your big corpus.

775
00:33:52,890 --> 00:33:54,570
You find similar things.

776
00:33:54,570 --> 00:33:58,380
You look at the words that come
next to the similar things.

777
00:33:58,380 --> 00:34:00,330
You rank that thing.

778
00:34:00,330 --> 00:34:01,830
You sample your top k.

779
00:34:01,830 --> 00:34:03,330
You renormalize that.

780
00:34:03,330 --> 00:34:05,580
So now you have a
bunch of scores,

781
00:34:05,580 --> 00:34:09,610
and now you can just interpolate
between your retrieved

782
00:34:09,610 --> 00:34:12,980
non-parametric memory scores and
your parametric language model

783
00:34:12,980 --> 00:34:13,480
scores.

784
00:34:13,480 --> 00:34:16,179
So this is very late
fusion in a sense.

785
00:34:16,179 --> 00:34:18,429
At the very end, you
combine these two,

786
00:34:18,429 --> 00:34:21,429
and it allows you to reweight
the pure language model

787
00:34:21,429 --> 00:34:24,100
probabilities or likelihoods.

788
00:34:24,100 --> 00:34:25,330
So this works really well.

789
00:34:25,330 --> 00:34:29,530
And it scales especially well
if you have a huge retrieval

790
00:34:29,530 --> 00:34:30,760
corpus.

791
00:34:30,760 --> 00:34:33,280
So if you have trillions and
trillions of tokens in there,

792
00:34:33,280 --> 00:34:35,469
you can have a much
smaller language model

793
00:34:35,469 --> 00:34:38,320
that does not that much heavy
lifting because you can really

794
00:34:38,320 --> 00:34:41,800
rely on this big source corpus
that you're working from.

795
00:34:41,800 --> 00:34:46,600
And so that idea was exploited
by this paper called "Retro"

796
00:34:46,600 --> 00:34:50,860
out of DeepMind, where they
showed that you can have a 25

797
00:34:50,860 --> 00:34:53,860
times smaller retrieval
augmented language model trained

798
00:34:53,860 --> 00:34:54,699
from scratch.

799
00:34:54,699 --> 00:34:57,610
So really pre-trained
entirely from scratch

800
00:34:57,610 --> 00:35:01,210
that outperforms this
25 times bigger language

801
00:35:01,210 --> 00:35:03,460
model on the same data
in terms of perplexity,

802
00:35:03,460 --> 00:35:05,290
which is pretty impressive.

803
00:35:05,290 --> 00:35:07,580
So this architecture
is much more efficient

804
00:35:07,580 --> 00:35:10,080
than a parametric
model because you can

805
00:35:10,080 --> 00:35:11,970
rely on this external memory.

806
00:35:11,970 --> 00:35:14,550
So if your external
memory is big enough,

807
00:35:14,550 --> 00:35:17,280
you can get pretty huge gains.

808
00:35:17,280 --> 00:35:19,650
So there was a lot of
excitement about RETRO

809
00:35:19,650 --> 00:35:22,530
when it was announced,
but it's a DeepMind paper.

810
00:35:22,530 --> 00:35:24,630
So there's really
no open-source.

811
00:35:24,630 --> 00:35:28,380
Nothing really to validate
that this actually works.

812
00:35:28,380 --> 00:35:32,430
And so very recently, there has
been a bit of work from NVIDIA

813
00:35:32,430 --> 00:35:38,160
called Retro++, where they have
this hybrid between the RETRO

814
00:35:38,160 --> 00:35:41,130
architecture, and then
they do basically RAG.

815
00:35:41,130 --> 00:35:43,890
They put the top one
or the top k results

816
00:35:43,890 --> 00:35:46,470
in the context of the
language model after all.

817
00:35:46,470 --> 00:35:49,980
So it's a crossover
between RAG and RETRO.

818
00:35:49,980 --> 00:35:51,960
And they showed some
really nice results here.

819
00:35:51,960 --> 00:35:56,490
But I think it's pointing
to this big flaw, I think,

820
00:35:56,490 --> 00:36:00,690
is that, why is there still no
good open-source RETRO model?

821
00:36:00,690 --> 00:36:03,330
That probably tells you
something about whether it

822
00:36:03,330 --> 00:36:04,470
actually really works.

823
00:36:04,470 --> 00:36:06,270
I spent a lot of
time in my career

824
00:36:06,270 --> 00:36:08,730
trying to reproduce
DeepMind papers that

825
00:36:08,730 --> 00:36:11,200
didn't necessarily always work.

826
00:36:11,200 --> 00:36:15,900
And so I think the
same is true for RETRO.

827
00:36:15,900 --> 00:36:18,570
And that's why we need
to do this in context RAG

828
00:36:18,570 --> 00:36:22,160
on top of RETRO to
actually get it to work.

829
00:36:22,160 --> 00:36:24,410
But could it just be
a true [INAUDIBLE]??

830
00:36:24,410 --> 00:36:28,580
Because you're searching
a trillion [INAUDIBLE]??

831
00:36:28,580 --> 00:36:30,170
Yeah, so--

832
00:36:30,170 --> 00:36:32,546
Deploying that
[INAUDIBLE] stuff?

833
00:36:32,546 --> 00:36:36,540
Yeah, so the doing retrieval
over that big corpus

834
00:36:36,540 --> 00:36:39,930
is not that difficult,
actually, yeah.

835
00:36:39,930 --> 00:36:43,080
So there are even like
distributed FAISS packages.

836
00:36:43,080 --> 00:36:44,850
You can just do
everything yourself.

837
00:36:44,850 --> 00:36:45,060
So--

838
00:36:45,060 --> 00:36:45,570
[INAUDIBLE]

839
00:36:45,570 --> 00:36:48,540
Yeah, so in terms
of compute, it's

840
00:36:48,540 --> 00:36:51,060
actually not that hard
anymore to reproduce something

841
00:36:51,060 --> 00:36:52,170
like this.

842
00:36:52,170 --> 00:36:57,100
But I tried several times, and
it is not really reproducible.

843
00:36:57,100 --> 00:36:58,660
So the only way
to get it to work

844
00:36:58,660 --> 00:37:01,420
is if you do this in context
RAG on top of the RETRO thing.

845
00:37:01,420 --> 00:37:03,490
And then as you can see
here in the results,

846
00:37:03,490 --> 00:37:06,550
then it actually gives you a
gain over the pure GPT model.

847
00:37:06,550 --> 00:37:08,050
So it starts from
a GPT, and then

848
00:37:08,050 --> 00:37:12,900
they retrofit as they
call it the GPT model.

849
00:37:12,900 --> 00:37:14,610
So in short, I think
there are still

850
00:37:14,610 --> 00:37:17,280
a lot of work to be done in
pre-training these systems

851
00:37:17,280 --> 00:37:18,660
really from scratch.

852
00:37:18,660 --> 00:37:20,790
And RETRO showed that
it might be possible,

853
00:37:20,790 --> 00:37:23,190
but we don't necessarily
know exactly how

854
00:37:23,190 --> 00:37:24,270
to do it the right way.

855
00:37:24,270 --> 00:37:29,040
And this is really one of the
interesting open questions.

856
00:37:29,040 --> 00:37:30,300
Any questions on that?

857
00:37:30,300 --> 00:37:34,540

858
00:37:34,540 --> 00:37:35,040
Online?

859
00:37:35,040 --> 00:37:39,730

860
00:37:39,730 --> 00:37:40,360
No?

861
00:37:40,360 --> 00:37:43,240
OK, then we'll move on.

862
00:37:43,240 --> 00:37:49,610
So let's go all the way
with the contextualization.

863
00:37:49,610 --> 00:37:54,080
So with RETRO and with RAG,
what we actually did is

864
00:37:54,080 --> 00:37:56,720
we only updated
the query encoder.

865
00:37:56,720 --> 00:38:01,710
So updating the document
encoder is very expensive.

866
00:38:01,710 --> 00:38:03,590
So one of the first
papers, actually,

867
00:38:03,590 --> 00:38:08,090
the OG of the non-frozen dense
retrieval augmented methods,

868
00:38:08,090 --> 00:38:10,280
is this paper called "REALM."

869
00:38:10,280 --> 00:38:12,260
This is really like
visionary work.

870
00:38:12,260 --> 00:38:17,750
This was basically the first
version that did this properly

871
00:38:17,750 --> 00:38:19,850
where they updated
it all the way,

872
00:38:19,850 --> 00:38:22,430
including the document encoder.

873
00:38:22,430 --> 00:38:24,740
So can someone
explain to me why it's

874
00:38:24,740 --> 00:38:26,810
expensive to update
the document encoder?

875
00:38:26,810 --> 00:38:30,970

876
00:38:30,970 --> 00:38:35,800
So let's say we have a
trillion tokens in our corpus.

877
00:38:35,800 --> 00:38:37,150
So now we go all the way.

878
00:38:37,150 --> 00:38:39,550
So we basically
do a forward pass.

879
00:38:39,550 --> 00:38:41,020
We get a gradient at the end.

880
00:38:41,020 --> 00:38:43,780
Now we back propagate the
gradient through the retriever.

881
00:38:43,780 --> 00:38:45,220
We update the query encoder.

882
00:38:45,220 --> 00:38:47,740
Now we have to update
the document encoder.

883
00:38:47,740 --> 00:38:49,720
So what do we then
need to do after we've

884
00:38:49,720 --> 00:38:51,700
updated the document encoder?

885
00:38:51,700 --> 00:38:54,730
We need to re-encode
the entire internet.

886
00:38:54,730 --> 00:38:57,160
So basically, every
single gradient update,

887
00:38:57,160 --> 00:39:00,100
we have to re-encode
whatever our index is.

888
00:39:00,100 --> 00:39:02,290
And so if this is like
trillions of tokens,

889
00:39:02,290 --> 00:39:04,930
it's like re-encoding the
internet after every batch

890
00:39:04,930 --> 00:39:05,950
update.

891
00:39:05,950 --> 00:39:09,500
So that's not very efficient.

892
00:39:09,500 --> 00:39:17,675
[INAUDIBLE] or other stuff
that if you [INAUDIBLE]

893
00:39:17,675 --> 00:39:21,300
take care of all the activations
and have some [INAUDIBLE]

894
00:39:21,300 --> 00:39:22,520
unpredictable thing.

895
00:39:22,520 --> 00:39:23,800
So [INAUDIBLE] this?

896
00:39:23,800 --> 00:39:24,300
Yeah.

897
00:39:24,300 --> 00:39:27,040

898
00:39:27,040 --> 00:39:29,140
Yeah, that's one way to do it.

899
00:39:29,140 --> 00:39:30,970
So there are a bunch
of different ways

900
00:39:30,970 --> 00:39:33,040
to update the document encoder.

901
00:39:33,040 --> 00:39:38,380
So what they do in REALM is they
basically do it for t batches.

902
00:39:38,380 --> 00:39:39,700
Then they stop.

903
00:39:39,700 --> 00:39:41,710
They re-encode the
entire internet,

904
00:39:41,710 --> 00:39:43,520
and then they train again.

905
00:39:43,520 --> 00:39:45,430
So it's sort of
asynchronous updates.

906
00:39:45,430 --> 00:39:48,460
They have this very fancy
sharding mechanisms,

907
00:39:48,460 --> 00:39:52,690
where they take down certain
parts of their entire index

908
00:39:52,690 --> 00:39:55,720
and then update them on the fly.

909
00:39:55,720 --> 00:39:56,680
So you can do it.

910
00:39:56,680 --> 00:39:57,760
It's just very expensive.

911
00:39:57,760 --> 00:39:59,680
So one of the things
that a lot of people

912
00:39:59,680 --> 00:40:02,200
have been thinking about,
not exactly the LoRa idea,

913
00:40:02,200 --> 00:40:06,290
but similar versions of
that are around like,

914
00:40:06,290 --> 00:40:08,290
can you make it more
efficient so that you don't

915
00:40:08,290 --> 00:40:12,740
have to do this asynchronously?

916
00:40:12,740 --> 00:40:16,250
So one of the downsides
of this REALM architecture

917
00:40:16,250 --> 00:40:18,328
is that it's really
just a bird model,

918
00:40:18,328 --> 00:40:20,120
but then you do this
retrieval augmentation

919
00:40:20,120 --> 00:40:21,770
on a bird model with
other bird models.

920
00:40:21,770 --> 00:40:23,020
So it's not really generative.

921
00:40:23,020 --> 00:40:26,120
It's not really gen AI
in the modern paradigm.

922
00:40:26,120 --> 00:40:30,080
But if you want to read
one paper on this topic,

923
00:40:30,080 --> 00:40:32,990
this is a very good one to read.

924
00:40:32,990 --> 00:40:36,770
The other one that is really,
really good to read is this

925
00:40:36,770 --> 00:40:38,510
paper called "Atlas."

926
00:40:38,510 --> 00:40:44,630
So Atlas is-- so this is out
of FAIR with a bunch of folks,

927
00:40:44,630 --> 00:40:49,280
the folks who did RAG and the
folks who did FID and really

928
00:40:49,280 --> 00:40:50,930
a brilliant set of people.

929
00:40:50,930 --> 00:40:54,500
And this is really a
comprehensive analysis

930
00:40:54,500 --> 00:40:57,050
of everything that's happening
in this architecture.

931
00:40:57,050 --> 00:40:58,850
So the first question
they really look at

932
00:40:58,850 --> 00:41:00,590
is, how do we train
this retriever?

933
00:41:00,590 --> 00:41:03,830
So we've seen a couple
of versions of this,

934
00:41:03,830 --> 00:41:06,445
but which one
actually works better?

935
00:41:06,445 --> 00:41:07,820
They haven't really
been compared

936
00:41:07,820 --> 00:41:09,500
in a head-to-head setting.

937
00:41:09,500 --> 00:41:14,040
So one thing is we have this FID
style attention distillation.

938
00:41:14,040 --> 00:41:17,700
So that's really too complicated
to go into detail here.

939
00:41:17,700 --> 00:41:20,220
But the others are
actually very simple.

940
00:41:20,220 --> 00:41:24,660
So one is this loss we've
basically seen before.

941
00:41:24,660 --> 00:41:28,170
So we've seen this, I think,
with the in-context RAG one.

942
00:41:28,170 --> 00:41:30,390
So we have a stop gradient
on the language model,

943
00:41:30,390 --> 00:41:32,430
and then we update
the retriever.

944
00:41:32,430 --> 00:41:35,280
The other one is what
we've seen with REPLUG.

945
00:41:35,280 --> 00:41:37,680
So this is basically
exactly the REPLUG loss.

946
00:41:37,680 --> 00:41:42,090
So we have the KL
divergence of the documents

947
00:41:42,090 --> 00:41:47,070
and the improvement that you see
when you give it that document.

948
00:41:47,070 --> 00:41:49,140
The other thing they
have is basically

949
00:41:49,140 --> 00:41:50,400
the inverse of that one.

950
00:41:50,400 --> 00:41:53,160
So if I take this
one document out,

951
00:41:53,160 --> 00:41:56,400
how does that
affect my perplexity

952
00:41:56,400 --> 00:41:59,010
of the language model?

953
00:41:59,010 --> 00:42:02,250
And so this one, I think,
is actually quite elegant

954
00:42:02,250 --> 00:42:04,770
because that really
gets to how valuable

955
00:42:04,770 --> 00:42:07,860
is this one single
document for me answering

956
00:42:07,860 --> 00:42:09,900
this question correctly.

957
00:42:09,900 --> 00:42:13,500
So they compare all of
these different versions.

958
00:42:13,500 --> 00:42:19,080
And what you can see is that the
REPLUG-style loss and this leave

959
00:42:19,080 --> 00:42:21,210
one out loss, they
performed a lot better

960
00:42:21,210 --> 00:42:22,260
than all of these others.

961
00:42:22,260 --> 00:42:25,500
So this fixed retriever or
no joint pre-training, these

962
00:42:25,500 --> 00:42:29,880
are really the baseline frozen
RAG models or close book.

963
00:42:29,880 --> 00:42:33,090
And as you can see, you
can do really a lot better

964
00:42:33,090 --> 00:42:35,430
if you optimize things.

965
00:42:35,430 --> 00:42:38,430
And so just leave one out
thing is probably the best,

966
00:42:38,430 --> 00:42:40,020
I would say.

967
00:42:40,020 --> 00:42:42,840
So then the other question
is, how do you actually

968
00:42:42,840 --> 00:42:44,460
train that entire system?

969
00:42:44,460 --> 00:42:46,860
What data or what tasks
do you train this on?

970
00:42:46,860 --> 00:42:50,670
So they also experiment with
a bunch of different versions.

971
00:42:50,670 --> 00:42:55,050
So one is doing PrefixLM, if
you're familiar with that.

972
00:42:55,050 --> 00:42:58,830
So they basically take a
chunk that occurs somewhere

973
00:42:58,830 --> 00:43:01,890
on the internet, and then
they predict the next chunk

974
00:43:01,890 --> 00:43:03,000
from that chunk.

975
00:43:03,000 --> 00:43:05,820
So it's really like
sentence to sentence.

976
00:43:05,820 --> 00:43:07,740
So maybe like Skip-Thought
back in the day,

977
00:43:07,740 --> 00:43:09,570
but now you have this
retrieval step where

978
00:43:09,570 --> 00:43:11,650
you predict the next sentence.

979
00:43:11,650 --> 00:43:14,645
And then they just do
T5 styles or denoising.

980
00:43:14,645 --> 00:43:16,020
So there's mass
language modeling

981
00:43:16,020 --> 00:43:18,270
if you're familiar with T5.

982
00:43:18,270 --> 00:43:21,330
And then they have this title
to section generation piece.

983
00:43:21,330 --> 00:43:23,940
So I think the takeaway
from this table

984
00:43:23,940 --> 00:43:26,220
is basically that
whatever you do here--

985
00:43:26,220 --> 00:43:28,260
so they're using T5 models.

986
00:43:28,260 --> 00:43:31,230
So whatever you do here needs to
be the same that your language

987
00:43:31,230 --> 00:43:33,060
model expects.

988
00:43:33,060 --> 00:43:36,930
So for T5, that's T5 style loss.

989
00:43:36,930 --> 00:43:40,680
And then the next final
question that they

990
00:43:40,680 --> 00:43:44,250
look into going back to what
we talked about, how exactly do

991
00:43:44,250 --> 00:43:46,080
we update this retriever?

992
00:43:46,080 --> 00:43:49,050
So do we have to update
the document encoder?

993
00:43:49,050 --> 00:43:52,230
Or do we maybe have
to do some reranking?

994
00:43:52,230 --> 00:43:54,870
Or do we maybe just
update the query?

995
00:43:54,870 --> 00:43:57,270
And quite surprisingly,
I think they find

996
00:43:57,270 --> 00:43:58,980
that just updating the query.

997
00:43:58,980 --> 00:44:01,620
So in the original
RAG paper, it's

998
00:44:01,620 --> 00:44:05,520
actually already basically
good enough in many cases.

999
00:44:05,520 --> 00:44:08,010
So that's nice because
it's much more efficient

1000
00:44:08,010 --> 00:44:11,550
if you don't have to update
your documents all the time.

1001
00:44:11,550 --> 00:44:14,520
I think the real question
here, though, is like,

1002
00:44:14,520 --> 00:44:18,390
how good is your document
representation to begin with?

1003
00:44:18,390 --> 00:44:21,150
So you need to have very, very
high quality embedding model

1004
00:44:21,150 --> 00:44:21,900
for this to work.

1005
00:44:21,900 --> 00:44:23,940
If you don't have that,
then this will not work.

1006
00:44:23,940 --> 00:44:27,480
