1255
00:55:53,590 --> 00:55:56,230
that's also very inefficient
because I probably don't have

1256
00:55:56,230 --> 00:55:59,110
to retrieve to generate the--

1257
00:55:59,110 --> 00:55:59,610
right?

1258
00:55:59,610 --> 00:56:01,902
I can probably do that on my
own with a language model.

1259
00:56:01,902 --> 00:56:04,480
It's a waste to go
and retrieve stuff.

1260
00:56:04,480 --> 00:56:07,950
But if I only retrieve once at
the beginning of the sequence,

1261
00:56:07,950 --> 00:56:10,200
that's probably also not great.

1262
00:56:10,200 --> 00:56:13,073
So what we ideally want to
be able to do is to say,

1263
00:56:13,073 --> 00:56:14,490
OK, sometimes, I
want to retrieve,

1264
00:56:14,490 --> 00:56:15,840
sometimes, I don't
want to retrieve.

1265
00:56:15,840 --> 00:56:17,340
And I'm going to
learn when I want

1266
00:56:17,340 --> 00:56:22,560
to expand the compute budget
on doing the retrieval.

1267
00:56:22,560 --> 00:56:25,560
So a nice paper where
they have a stab at this

1268
00:56:25,560 --> 00:56:28,290
is called FLARE for active
retrieval augmentation

1269
00:56:28,290 --> 00:56:31,560
where they basically have
the language model decide

1270
00:56:31,560 --> 00:56:36,990
when it should do a search and
what it should do to search for.

1271
00:56:36,990 --> 00:56:39,690
So I think this fits
in a general trend

1272
00:56:39,690 --> 00:56:42,540
that you can see in the
field around agents.

1273
00:56:42,540 --> 00:56:45,690
So we can talk a little
bit more about that too.

1274
00:56:45,690 --> 00:56:48,870
So this other
question that I think

1275
00:56:48,870 --> 00:56:52,260
we also covered already here is
how do we train this at scale?

1276
00:56:52,260 --> 00:56:54,210
So we can do these
asynchronous updates.

1277
00:56:54,210 --> 00:56:55,260
We can do rerankers.

1278
00:56:55,260 --> 00:56:57,000
We can do query side only.

1279
00:56:57,000 --> 00:56:59,550
There's this really
nice paper which

1280
00:56:59,550 --> 00:57:03,240
is quite close I think to
the idea you proposed where

1281
00:57:03,240 --> 00:57:08,190
you first use BM25 to create a
batch basically where everything

1282
00:57:08,190 --> 00:57:11,730
is very similar in terms
of what you retrieved

1283
00:57:11,730 --> 00:57:15,960
and now you have
this in-batch update.

1284
00:57:15,960 --> 00:57:18,960
So it's like a reranker where
you encode the information that

1285
00:57:18,960 --> 00:57:21,870
is just in your batch
using this other model

1286
00:57:21,870 --> 00:57:24,210
and now you can update
this model on the fly,

1287
00:57:24,210 --> 00:57:26,220
so you don't have to
worry too much about doing

1288
00:57:26,220 --> 00:57:29,430
the full document side update.

1289
00:57:29,430 --> 00:57:31,110
And again, here,
what really matters

1290
00:57:31,110 --> 00:57:32,520
is like how big is your index.

1291
00:57:32,520 --> 00:57:34,050
If you have an
amazing index, you

1292
00:57:34,050 --> 00:57:37,470
can basically solve any
problem just by looking it up.

1293
00:57:37,470 --> 00:57:39,870
So rather than cramming
it into your parameters,

1294
00:57:39,870 --> 00:57:41,130
you can just find it.

1295
00:57:41,130 --> 00:57:43,960

1296
00:57:43,960 --> 00:57:46,960
This is a really nice
paper called SILO.

1297
00:57:46,960 --> 00:57:49,600
So one of the
interesting things I

1298
00:57:49,600 --> 00:57:52,548
think that's going to happen
in the next year or two

1299
00:57:52,548 --> 00:57:55,090
around language models is there,
and you've seen this already

1300
00:57:55,090 --> 00:57:57,400
there's a bunch of
lawsuits against OpenAI

1301
00:57:57,400 --> 00:58:01,840
and other places around where
does the data exactly come from.

1302
00:58:01,840 --> 00:58:04,990
So one very elegant
solution I think

1303
00:58:04,990 --> 00:58:07,000
is to have a system
that you train

1304
00:58:07,000 --> 00:58:11,800
on data that is safe so you can
train that thing on Wikipedia.

1305
00:58:11,800 --> 00:58:14,200
But now during test
time, you can give it

1306
00:58:14,200 --> 00:58:17,170
a data store that has
maybe slightly riskier

1307
00:58:17,170 --> 00:58:20,050
information in it so
this massive index

1308
00:58:20,050 --> 00:58:22,840
of all the stuff on the internet
including some things that

1309
00:58:22,840 --> 00:58:26,020
are maybe higher risk.

1310
00:58:26,020 --> 00:58:29,620
You can still have them in your
index but your language model,

1311
00:58:29,620 --> 00:58:31,918
your retrieval augmented
language model, I should say,

1312
00:58:31,918 --> 00:58:34,210
you know that that thing is
safe because it was trained

1313
00:58:34,210 --> 00:58:36,280
on data that is public domain.

1314
00:58:36,280 --> 00:58:38,230
So that's what they do
in SILO and they show

1315
00:58:38,230 --> 00:58:39,670
that that works really well.

1316
00:58:39,670 --> 00:58:45,050
So that's one possible solution
to a lot of the compliance

1317
00:58:45,050 --> 00:58:49,510
and legal risk around
language model deployments.

1318
00:58:49,510 --> 00:58:53,080
There's a great paper
on also from one

1319
00:58:53,080 --> 00:58:56,245
of your colleagues
around context

1320
00:58:56,245 --> 00:58:57,370
getting lost in the middle.

1321
00:58:57,370 --> 00:58:59,680
I think this is also a
fascinating phenomenon.

1322
00:58:59,680 --> 00:59:02,230
This is on a frozen RAG system.

1323
00:59:02,230 --> 00:59:07,840
But language models are very
similar to humans in what things

1324
00:59:07,840 --> 00:59:09,250
they pay attention to.

1325
00:59:09,250 --> 00:59:12,250
So if you give them a bunch
of things that you retrieve,

1326
00:59:12,250 --> 00:59:15,070
what they will look at are
the first things you list

1327
00:59:15,070 --> 00:59:16,330
and the last things you list.

1328
00:59:16,330 --> 00:59:18,730
And they will ignore the middle.

1329
00:59:18,730 --> 00:59:21,430
And so if it actually
respected the rank function,

1330
00:59:21,430 --> 00:59:23,980
then this curve would go
down all the way, right?

1331
00:59:23,980 --> 00:59:26,320
But it goes up.

1332
00:59:26,320 --> 00:59:30,460
So I think that's a very
interesting observation which

1333
00:59:30,460 --> 00:59:34,540
shows that how brittle
these systems can be.

1334
00:59:34,540 --> 00:59:36,370
So if you have a
frozen RAG system,

1335
00:59:36,370 --> 00:59:38,200
it can be very,
very brittle where

1336
00:59:38,200 --> 00:59:40,270
like the order of
the retrieve context

1337
00:59:40,270 --> 00:59:44,582
matters a lot in whether you
get the right answer or not.

1338
00:59:44,582 --> 00:59:47,910
Can you then work on reading
this as a reinforcement learning

1339
00:59:47,910 --> 00:59:50,790
problem in a sense
of place and vector

1340
00:59:50,790 --> 00:59:54,570
as opposed to specifically
going for interpretation,

1341
00:59:54,570 --> 00:59:57,360
try to output a vector
that's going to inner product

1342
00:59:57,360 --> 00:59:59,820
with just the right--

1343
00:59:59,820 --> 01:00:02,490
maybe you can tune for
a particular database?

1344
01:00:02,490 --> 01:00:03,180
Yeah.

1345
01:00:03,180 --> 01:00:04,630
I don't know exactly.

1346
01:00:04,630 --> 01:00:05,130
Yeah.

1347
01:00:05,130 --> 01:00:07,800
So what I just described
somebody asked,

1348
01:00:07,800 --> 01:00:09,518
like how do you
actually-- so I said

1349
01:00:09,518 --> 01:00:12,060
there are other ways to do this
and then the question was how

1350
01:00:12,060 --> 01:00:12,850
do you do that?

1351
01:00:12,850 --> 01:00:16,320
So the way you do that
is using reinforce.

1352
01:00:16,320 --> 01:00:19,650
So yeah, there has been
work on doing that.

1353
01:00:19,650 --> 01:00:21,960
So some of the older papers
were playing with this.

1354
01:00:21,960 --> 01:00:25,290
But one of the big
problems with--

1355
01:00:25,290 --> 01:00:27,390
so I think the
REPLUG solution is

1356
01:00:27,390 --> 01:00:31,050
more elegant for
solving that problem

1357
01:00:31,050 --> 01:00:33,990
because you actually use
signal from the language model.

1358
01:00:33,990 --> 01:00:36,420
And if you just do reinforce,
it's very high variance.

1359
01:00:36,420 --> 01:00:40,320
So you're it's going to be
super finicky if you don't

1360
01:00:40,320 --> 01:00:43,250
want to destroy your index.

1361
01:00:43,250 --> 01:00:44,636
But people have tried it, yeah.

1362
01:00:44,636 --> 01:00:48,740

1363
01:00:48,740 --> 01:00:53,390
So there are some really
nice work from OpenAI

1364
01:00:53,390 --> 01:00:55,460
where they basically show--

1365
01:00:55,460 --> 01:00:59,000
and again, we're like thinking
more and more about agents here,

1366
01:00:59,000 --> 01:01:00,110
right?

1367
01:01:00,110 --> 01:01:02,870
Where they show something very
similar to the FLARE results

1368
01:01:02,870 --> 01:01:04,430
from earlier with
active retrieval

1369
01:01:04,430 --> 01:01:07,220
that doesn't necessarily have
to be some index that you own.

1370
01:01:07,220 --> 01:01:10,487
It can be just some
web search, right?

1371
01:01:10,487 --> 01:01:12,320
And obviously in this
case, you don't really

1372
01:01:12,320 --> 01:01:15,020
have access to the web
search necessarily, so Bing

1373
01:01:15,020 --> 01:01:18,440
or whatever they use here is not
going to update its parameters.

1374
01:01:18,440 --> 01:01:21,200
But I just wanted to
put this in your mind,

1375
01:01:21,200 --> 01:01:23,900
like this is another
thing you can do, right?

1376
01:01:23,900 --> 01:01:27,290
And if we take this really
to the general form,

1377
01:01:27,290 --> 01:01:30,920
then you can think of language
models as just tool users.

1378
01:01:30,920 --> 01:01:34,220
So rather than just retrieval
augmenting language models,

1379
01:01:34,220 --> 01:01:37,190
we can tool augment language
models and retrieval is just

1380
01:01:37,190 --> 01:01:40,190
one of the many tools that
language models have access to.

1381
01:01:40,190 --> 01:01:42,950
We can have rerankers
and things on top

1382
01:01:42,950 --> 01:01:45,150
of the outputs of these tools.

1383
01:01:45,150 --> 01:01:47,780
And so one of the big
questions, I think,

1384
01:01:47,780 --> 01:01:51,620
is, how do you actually get this
system to learn stuff, right?

1385
01:01:51,620 --> 01:01:54,620
So we're going to need RL if we
want this system to really learn

1386
01:01:54,620 --> 01:01:56,570
how to take these
actions properly.

1387
01:01:56,570 --> 01:01:59,780

1388
01:01:59,780 --> 01:02:03,620
And so yeah, this has
been taken to the extreme

1389
01:02:03,620 --> 01:02:07,400
and is self RAG architecture
where they have this retrieval

1390
01:02:07,400 --> 01:02:09,620
step and it's active and
then you criticize it

1391
01:02:09,620 --> 01:02:13,280
and then you basically do some
natural language inference

1392
01:02:13,280 --> 01:02:15,290
and all of that just
with one language model

1393
01:02:15,290 --> 01:02:18,630
to answer the questions.

1394
01:02:18,630 --> 01:02:21,470
So the other missing
piece-- so I'm just

1395
01:02:21,470 --> 01:02:23,630
going through a bunch
of open questions

1396
01:02:23,630 --> 01:02:26,270
that people have
looked at but feel

1397
01:02:26,270 --> 01:02:29,270
free to interrupt me if there's
anything you want to know.

1398
01:02:29,270 --> 01:02:31,852
But so instruction
tuning, we established

1399
01:02:31,852 --> 01:02:33,560
at the beginning of
the lecture that this

1400
01:02:33,560 --> 01:02:35,690
is pretty important for
getting things to work, so

1401
01:02:35,690 --> 01:02:38,240
fixing the user interface.

1402
01:02:38,240 --> 01:02:41,750
But the instruction
tuning has almost always

1403
01:02:41,750 --> 01:02:43,250
only happened on
the language model

1404
01:02:43,250 --> 01:02:44,900
and not on the entire system.

1405
01:02:44,900 --> 01:02:47,180
So I think one of the
interesting things

1406
01:02:47,180 --> 01:02:49,970
that people are looking at
now with things like raw data

1407
01:02:49,970 --> 01:02:52,370
and InstructRetro is how can
we instruct and fine tune

1408
01:02:52,370 --> 01:02:54,410
an entire retrieval
augmented system.

1409
01:02:54,410 --> 01:02:57,080
So all the way into
the retrieval step

1410
01:02:57,080 --> 01:02:59,340
can we generate
data so that also

1411
01:02:59,340 --> 01:03:00,953
follows the
instructions properly

1412
01:03:00,953 --> 01:03:03,120
which currently doesn't
happen in any of these model

1413
01:03:03,120 --> 01:03:05,880
architectures.

1414
01:03:05,880 --> 01:03:08,430
And then finally, I
think I would be remiss

1415
01:03:08,430 --> 01:03:12,480
if I didn't really talk about
what people call advanced RAGs.

1416
01:03:12,480 --> 01:03:14,910
So like the developer
community has been really doing

1417
01:03:14,910 --> 01:03:17,940
some awesome stuff,
so like frameworks,

1418
01:03:17,940 --> 01:03:19,530
like LlamaIndex, and LangChain.

1419
01:03:19,530 --> 01:03:21,870
And there's all these open
source vector databases

1420
01:03:21,870 --> 01:03:23,190
like Chroma and Weaviate.

1421
01:03:23,190 --> 01:03:26,100
And they're all about
making RAGs really easy.

1422
01:03:26,100 --> 01:03:28,590
But this is all
frozen RAG, right?

1423
01:03:28,590 --> 01:03:32,130
But even with frozen RAG, you
can really do incredible things.

1424
01:03:32,130 --> 01:03:34,800
So we mentioned some
of these already.

1425
01:03:34,800 --> 01:03:36,660
So child-parent
recursive retriever.

1426
01:03:36,660 --> 01:03:40,260
So you find small parts and then
you give the big parts around it

1427
01:03:40,260 --> 01:03:41,460
to the language model.

1428
01:03:41,460 --> 01:03:44,850
You can do hybrid search where
we use reciprocal rank fusion,

1429
01:03:44,850 --> 01:03:46,680
so we have different
search results

1430
01:03:46,680 --> 01:03:49,470
that we didn't combine before
we give the final thing

1431
01:03:49,470 --> 01:03:51,000
to the language model.

1432
01:03:51,000 --> 01:03:53,820
There's zero shot like large
language model reranker,

1433
01:03:53,820 --> 01:03:55,470
so basically the
score function is

1434
01:03:55,470 --> 01:03:57,270
not-- doesn't come
from your retrieval,

1435
01:03:57,270 --> 01:03:59,880
it comes directly from
the language model.

1436
01:03:59,880 --> 01:04:03,090
And then hypothetical document
embeddings which I think

1437
01:04:03,090 --> 01:04:04,120
is a really cool idea.

1438
01:04:04,120 --> 01:04:07,860
So you just basically
you fix hallucination

1439
01:04:07,860 --> 01:04:09,660
through hallucination.

1440
01:04:09,660 --> 01:04:11,520
So you get a
question, then you let

1441
01:04:11,520 --> 01:04:14,250
the language model hallucinate
a bunch of possible answers,

1442
01:04:14,250 --> 01:04:16,620
then you go and search
for nearest neighbors

1443
01:04:16,620 --> 01:04:19,290
to the possible answers, and
you give those as context

1444
01:04:19,290 --> 01:04:22,120
and then it gives the
right answer based on that.

1445
01:04:22,120 --> 01:04:24,300
So it was really like
hallucinating answers.

1446
01:04:24,300 --> 01:04:27,880
And I think it's a
brilliant solution.

1447
01:04:27,880 --> 01:04:29,370
So there's a lot
of stuff happening

1448
01:04:29,370 --> 01:04:32,880
in the frozen RAG
community too that I think

1449
01:04:32,880 --> 01:04:35,580
is very interesting to look at.

1450
01:04:35,580 --> 01:04:41,490
So just to wrap up, looking
at the future of this stuff.

1451
01:04:41,490 --> 01:04:44,590
There are still lots of very
interesting open questions.

1452
01:04:44,590 --> 01:04:48,270
So if you're a student thinking
about how to solve any of these,

1453
01:04:48,270 --> 01:04:51,360
I think you can have
quite a lot of impact.

1454
01:04:51,360 --> 01:04:55,050
So how exactly do we
do like pre-training

1455
01:04:55,050 --> 01:04:55,980
of this architecture?

1456
01:04:55,980 --> 01:04:57,600
And do we even
need to pre-train?

1457
01:04:57,600 --> 01:05:00,535
I think even retro shows
that you don't necessarily

1458
01:05:00,535 --> 01:05:02,160
have to pre-train,
so but maybe there's

1459
01:05:02,160 --> 01:05:05,400
something wrong
with how we do that.

1460
01:05:05,400 --> 01:05:07,240
What do scaling laws look like?

1461
01:05:07,240 --> 01:05:09,760
So I think there's a really
interesting question here around

1462
01:05:09,760 --> 01:05:13,000
if I have a huge index and
a very rich encoder of all

1463
01:05:13,000 --> 01:05:16,120
the information in that
index, maybe I can move,

1464
01:05:16,120 --> 01:05:19,510
so basically decouple all the
memorization to this index.

1465
01:05:19,510 --> 01:05:21,940
So I have a language model
that doesn't know anything,

1466
01:05:21,940 --> 01:05:24,940
it just speaks English, it
just reasons on top of it.

1467
01:05:24,940 --> 01:05:26,650
It has no knowledge
because that always

1468
01:05:26,650 --> 01:05:28,030
comes from this retriever.

1469
01:05:28,030 --> 01:05:29,500
If you can do
something like that,

1470
01:05:29,500 --> 01:05:31,833
then you get very interesting
scaling trade offs, right?

1471
01:05:31,833 --> 01:05:33,700
So you can have a
tiny language model

1472
01:05:33,700 --> 01:05:37,570
and do your retrieval to do
a lot of the heavy lifting

1473
01:05:37,570 --> 01:05:39,970
with your retrieval which
is nice because that's

1474
01:05:39,970 --> 01:05:41,290
a cached computation, right?

1475
01:05:41,290 --> 01:05:43,900
So you can just-- you
already have the embeddings,

1476
01:05:43,900 --> 01:05:46,150
you just need to
do the dot product,

1477
01:05:46,150 --> 01:05:49,120
so it's much more efficient than
self-attention in the language

1478
01:05:49,120 --> 01:05:50,920
model.

1479
01:05:50,920 --> 01:05:52,630
Can we move beyond by encoders?

1480
01:05:52,630 --> 01:05:56,410
So vector databases,
I like people

1481
01:05:56,410 --> 01:05:58,540
who build vector
databases, but I'm not sure

1482
01:05:58,540 --> 01:06:01,660
how long we're going to
keep vector databases.

1483
01:06:01,660 --> 01:06:06,560
Because I think reranker is
probably work just as well.

1484
01:06:06,560 --> 01:06:10,780
And BM25 is much more efficient
than a vector database.

1485
01:06:10,780 --> 01:06:16,300
So I don't really see why we
need dedicated vector databases.

1486
01:06:16,300 --> 01:06:17,740
And so what we're
seeing but maybe

1487
01:06:17,740 --> 01:06:22,110
this is a bit of a critique of
maybe Silicon Valley investment

1488
01:06:22,110 --> 01:06:23,860
strategies and things
like that, but a lot

1489
01:06:23,860 --> 01:06:27,253
of these vector
database companies

1490
01:06:27,253 --> 01:06:29,170
are basically becoming
database companies now.

1491
01:06:29,170 --> 01:06:31,000
So they are adding
all this spark stuff

1492
01:06:31,000 --> 01:06:33,700
because the dense
thing is not enough.

1493
01:06:33,700 --> 01:06:37,570
And as it turns out, there are
a lot of pretty good sparse

1494
01:06:37,570 --> 01:06:40,600
databases out there already like
Postgres and things like that.

1495
01:06:40,600 --> 01:06:44,080
And they're also all adding
vectors to their databases

1496
